1
00:00:01,368 --> 00:00:04,272
♪ In the not too
distant future ♪

2
00:00:04,305 --> 00:00:07,342
♪ Next Sunday, A.D. ♪

3
00:00:07,375 --> 00:00:10,445
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:10,478 --> 00:00:13,948
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:13,982 --> 00:00:16,683
♪ He worked at
Gizmotic Institute ♪

6
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:20,122
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:20,155 --> 00:00:22,857
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,890 --> 00:00:24,358
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:24,392 --> 00:00:29,197
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:32,333
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

11
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:34,335
♪ The worst we can find ♪

12
00:00:34,368 --> 00:00:35,670
♪ La, la, la ♪

13
00:00:35,703 --> 00:00:38,238
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:38,272 --> 00:00:40,508
♪ And we'll monitor his mind ♪

15
00:00:40,542 --> 00:00:41,843
♪ La, la, la ♪

16
00:00:41,877 --> 00:00:44,345
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

17
00:00:44,378 --> 00:00:46,613
♪ Where the movies
begin or end ♪

18
00:00:46,647 --> 00:00:47,848
♪ La, la, la ♪

19
00:00:47,882 --> 00:00:50,785
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

20
00:00:50,818 --> 00:00:54,455
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

21
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:57,292
♪ Robot roll call ♪
♪ All right, let's go ♪

22
00:00:57,325 --> 00:00:58,392
♪ Cambot ♪
♪ Pan left ♪

23
00:00:58,427 --> 00:00:59,927
♪ Gypsy ♪
♪ Hi girl ♪

24
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:01,362
♪ Tom Servo ♪
♪ What a cool guy ♪

25
00:01:01,395 --> 00:01:03,230
♪ Crow ♪
♪ He's a wise cracker ♪

26
00:01:03,263 --> 00:01:06,167
♪ If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes ♪

27
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:08,135
♪ And other science facts ♪

28
00:01:08,168 --> 00:01:09,603
♪ La, la, la ♪

29
00:01:09,637 --> 00:01:12,173
♪ Then repeat to yourself,
it's just a show ♪

30
00:01:12,207 --> 00:01:15,410
♪ I should really just relax ♪

31
00:01:15,443 --> 00:01:19,680
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000 ♪

32
00:01:21,115 --> 00:01:23,985
(mysterious music)

33
00:01:31,558 --> 00:01:34,728
(audience applauding)

34
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,567
- Cambot, you don't have to
put applause in now, okay.

35
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:40,268
But you could bring
it in a little bit.

36
00:01:40,302 --> 00:01:41,102
Go ahead.

37
00:01:41,135 --> 00:01:41,937
Come on in.

38
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:43,203
A little bit more.

39
00:01:43,237 --> 00:01:44,538
That's good, okay.

40
00:01:44,572 --> 00:01:45,973
People of Earth,

41
00:01:46,007 --> 00:01:50,345
my name is Joel and I'm
marooned in outer space.

42
00:01:50,378 --> 00:01:53,482
- [Announcer] Commercial
starting in 15 seconds.

43
00:01:53,515 --> 00:01:57,085
- I'm the subject of a bizarre
movie watching experiment,

44
00:01:57,118 --> 00:01:59,220
and now, I guess, so are you.

45
00:01:59,253 --> 00:02:01,188
I'm expecting a
call any second now

46
00:02:01,222 --> 00:02:03,625
from the two evil scientists
who shot me into space

47
00:02:03,658 --> 00:02:04,892
for no good reason at all.

48
00:02:04,925 --> 00:02:08,095
- [Announcer] Commercial
starting in five, four,

49
00:02:08,128 --> 00:02:11,765
three, two, commercial
starting now.

50
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:12,733
(Joel hitting button)

51
00:02:12,767 --> 00:02:13,768
- Stick around.

52
00:02:13,802 --> 00:02:14,802
We'll be right back.

53
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:18,973
(upbeat music)

54
00:02:23,477 --> 00:02:25,780
- You know, one of
the really hard things

55
00:02:25,813 --> 00:02:27,682
about being marooned
in outer space

56
00:02:27,715 --> 00:02:29,483
is you start to miss
the little things

57
00:02:29,517 --> 00:02:30,952
that you have
every day on Earth,

58
00:02:30,985 --> 00:02:35,022
you know, like rain, and
snow, and mini marshmallows.

59
00:02:35,055 --> 00:02:36,892
You know, that's why
these vacuum flowers

60
00:02:36,925 --> 00:02:37,825
are important to me.

61
00:02:37,858 --> 00:02:39,360
They're like the only living,

62
00:02:39,393 --> 00:02:42,997
oh, I'm being called by
my two evil overlords.

63
00:02:43,030 --> 00:02:47,902
- Come in, Joel, mate, my
spunky little space sock.

64
00:02:47,935 --> 00:02:49,803
- Hey sirs, what's going on?

65
00:02:49,837 --> 00:02:51,605
- It's time for the
invention exchange.

66
00:02:51,638 --> 00:02:54,275
What have you got
for us, skanky boy?

67
00:02:54,308 --> 00:02:56,010
- Yeah, I got this
new thing here.

68
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:58,913
It's a, looks like an
ordinary circular saw,

69
00:02:58,947 --> 00:03:00,448
but it's actually a safety saw.

70
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:02,250
See, it's got a
little infrared light

71
00:03:02,283 --> 00:03:05,085
that senses when your
appendages or fingers are near,

72
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:06,922
it shuts itself
off automatically.

73
00:03:06,955 --> 00:03:11,492
(saw cranking)
I'll show you what I mean.

74
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:14,128
(saw whirring)

75
00:03:16,563 --> 00:03:19,000
(Joel screaming)

76
00:03:19,033 --> 00:03:21,335
I'm just kidding, it's a trick.

77
00:03:22,303 --> 00:03:24,738
Fake fingers.

78
00:03:24,772 --> 00:03:26,573
- Oh yes, we knew that.

79
00:03:26,607 --> 00:03:28,108
Well, it's our turn

80
00:03:28,142 --> 00:03:31,478
for our little experimental
house party this week, Joel.

81
00:03:31,512 --> 00:03:34,048
- Okay, say you want
to emulate Reed Richard

82
00:03:34,082 --> 00:03:35,317
from the Fantastic Four,

83
00:03:35,350 --> 00:03:36,883
and you need a longer
arm to reach something

84
00:03:36,918 --> 00:03:38,118
or change the channel,

85
00:03:38,152 --> 00:03:39,487
just use our limb
lengthener here.

86
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:42,623
- Yes, just select
the appropriate limb.

87
00:03:42,657 --> 00:03:44,858
In this case, Larry's
volunteered his arm.

88
00:03:44,892 --> 00:03:45,660
Comfy?

89
00:03:45,693 --> 00:03:46,493
(Larry grunting)

90
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:47,328
Yes, all right.

91
00:03:47,362 --> 00:03:48,495
And just stretch away.

92
00:03:48,528 --> 00:03:52,267
(Larry yelping and groaning)

93
00:03:53,768 --> 00:03:56,537
Oh it doesn't hurt that
much, you whiny little baby.

94
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:58,072
(Larry grunting)

95
00:03:58,105 --> 00:04:00,040
- Well, I have a feeling
you're probably violating

96
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:04,312
somebody's basic human
rights, namely Dr. Erhardt's.

97
00:04:04,345 --> 00:04:05,513
- [Both] Thank you.

98
00:04:05,547 --> 00:04:06,948
- Well, speaking of violations,

99
00:04:06,982 --> 00:04:08,683
here's a little
cinematic intrusion,

100
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:10,452
called The Crawling Hand.

101
00:04:10,485 --> 00:04:12,553
- It has a star from
Gilligan's Island,

102
00:04:12,587 --> 00:04:13,755
and one from The Big Valley,

103
00:04:13,788 --> 00:04:16,157
and one the Hamburger
Helper commercial.

104
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:17,658
- Yes.

105
00:04:17,692 --> 00:04:19,160
(both grunting)

106
00:04:19,193 --> 00:04:20,027
- Enjoy.

107
00:04:20,895 --> 00:04:22,330
- Oh brother.

108
00:04:22,363 --> 00:04:23,530
(alarm buzzing)

109
00:04:23,563 --> 00:04:24,498
Movie sign.

110
00:04:26,533 --> 00:04:29,437
(mysterious music)

111
00:04:44,118 --> 00:04:46,220
- [Tom] The Crawling Hand.

112
00:04:46,253 --> 00:04:47,055
Hey Joel.

113
00:04:47,088 --> 00:04:48,455
- [Joel] Hi.

114
00:04:48,488 --> 00:04:49,523
- [Crow] How'd the
scientists like your deal?

115
00:04:49,557 --> 00:04:50,758
- [Joel] Okay.

116
00:04:50,792 --> 00:04:52,193
They're crazier than
ever though, you know.

117
00:04:52,227 --> 00:04:53,460
- [Crow] Yeah.

118
00:04:53,493 --> 00:04:54,395
- [Tom] They're kind of
dickweeds, aren't they?

119
00:04:54,428 --> 00:04:55,697
- [Joel] You could say that.

120
00:04:55,730 --> 00:04:58,667
(mysterious music)

121
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:02,003
- [Tom] This is like
Love, American Style.

122
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:07,508
- [Crow] Hey, Alan Hale,
isn't that The Skipper?

123
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:08,375
- [Joel] Yeah.

124
00:05:11,612 --> 00:05:12,447
Little Buddy.

125
00:05:16,417 --> 00:05:19,820
I can see my house
from here, you guys.

126
00:05:19,853 --> 00:05:21,622
- [Crow] Hey, Joel, did
they do these titles

127
00:05:21,655 --> 00:05:22,890
on a typewriter?

128
00:05:22,923 --> 00:05:25,893
- [Joel] No, that's the
helvetica constellation

129
00:05:25,927 --> 00:05:26,893
we're looking at.

130
00:05:26,927 --> 00:05:27,762
- [Crow] Oh.

131
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:29,997
Stellar.

132
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,135
- [Tom] Is Artie Shaw in this?

133
00:05:34,168 --> 00:05:35,470
- [Joel] No, I don't think so.

134
00:05:35,503 --> 00:05:36,737
- [Crow] Just off camera.

135
00:05:38,005 --> 00:05:40,775
(dramatic music)

136
00:05:45,513 --> 00:05:48,182
- [Tom] (groaning) Scary.

137
00:05:49,883 --> 00:05:52,487
(Crow moaning)

138
00:05:54,388 --> 00:05:55,690
- [Lockhart] Do you read me?

139
00:05:55,723 --> 00:05:56,957
Do you read me?

140
00:05:56,990 --> 00:05:58,125
- [Tom] Like a book.

141
00:05:58,158 --> 00:05:59,593
- [Lockhart] Someone,
please, come in.

142
00:05:59,627 --> 00:06:00,728
Oh my god.

143
00:06:00,762 --> 00:06:02,897
- [Joel] It's Kirk Douglas.

144
00:06:02,930 --> 00:06:04,398
- [Tom] Anytown, USA.

145
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:09,203
- [Announcer] Dr. Weitzberg,
contact range officer.

146
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:14,242
Paging system now secured
for emergency use only.

147
00:06:14,275 --> 00:06:16,043
- [Crow] Unless you have
a note from your parents.

148
00:06:16,077 --> 00:06:18,678
- [Announcer] 46 hours,
20 minutes, zero seconds.

149
00:06:19,847 --> 00:06:21,748
- [Crow] Boy, he
really needs a smoke.

150
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:25,352
- Space Operations,
Florida division.

151
00:06:26,687 --> 00:06:27,555
Hold on.

152
00:06:28,455 --> 00:06:29,357
It's the press.

153
00:06:29,390 --> 00:06:30,290
- [Tom] Tell 'em I'm smoking.

154
00:06:30,357 --> 00:06:31,692
- What do we tell them, Steve?

155
00:06:31,758 --> 00:06:33,660
- [Tom] Tell 'em I'm smoking.

156
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:34,562
- I'll tell you
what to tell 'em.

157
00:06:34,595 --> 00:06:35,797
- [Tom] Tell 'em I'm smoking.

158
00:06:35,863 --> 00:06:37,598
- Tell 'em we sent our
second man to the moon.

159
00:06:37,665 --> 00:06:39,567
Looks like he's not
coming back either.

160
00:06:40,968 --> 00:06:42,403
Oh, I'm sorry Donna, I
shouldn't have said that.

161
00:06:42,470 --> 00:06:43,570
- [Crow] Oops.
- [Tom] I'm sorry.

162
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:44,872
Just tell 'em I'm smoking.

163
00:06:46,307 --> 00:06:47,708
He's smoking.

164
00:06:47,775 --> 00:06:49,877
- We can release no
statement at this time.

165
00:06:51,678 --> 00:06:52,647
- Oxygen countdown.

166
00:06:54,315 --> 00:06:56,817
- Mr. Curan would like a
countdown on the oxygen,

167
00:06:56,850 --> 00:06:58,685
every 15 seconds.

168
00:06:58,718 --> 00:06:59,920
- [Crow] And an ashtray.

169
00:07:01,155 --> 00:07:03,523
- [Announcer] Two
minutes, 30 seconds.

170
00:07:03,557 --> 00:07:06,460
- No contact for
nine full hours.

171
00:07:07,695 --> 00:07:09,463
(chair crashing)

172
00:07:09,497 --> 00:07:11,065
- [Tom] Goll.
- [Crow] Get him some decaf.

173
00:07:11,098 --> 00:07:13,233
- Same thing all over again.

174
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:15,670
- [Joel] Too many years
at the Strasberg school.

175
00:07:16,770 --> 00:07:17,938
- Same thing all over again.

176
00:07:17,972 --> 00:07:19,540
- [Joel] Isn't that Nick
from The Big Valley?

177
00:07:19,573 --> 00:07:20,775
- [Crow] Yeah.

178
00:07:20,808 --> 00:07:22,008
- Yet the whole rigs
clicking like a dream.

179
00:07:22,042 --> 00:07:23,912
- [Announcer] Two
minutes, 15 seconds.

180
00:07:23,945 --> 00:07:25,647
- [Tom] And 62 bottles
of beer on the wall.

181
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:26,980
- We licked every problem.

182
00:07:27,882 --> 00:07:29,250
Put a man on the moon.

183
00:07:30,518 --> 00:07:32,220
No, we got him off.

184
00:07:32,253 --> 00:07:34,022
Everything worked fine,

185
00:07:34,055 --> 00:07:35,422
but Lockhart.

186
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:36,523
What happened to him?

187
00:07:37,925 --> 00:07:38,860
- [Announcer] Two minutes,

188
00:07:38,893 --> 00:07:42,197
zero seconds.
(phone buzzing)

189
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:43,297
- Mr. Curan's desk.

190
00:07:44,965 --> 00:07:46,000
Yes, sir, one moment.

191
00:07:47,302 --> 00:07:48,168
- [Tom] He's smoking.

192
00:07:48,202 --> 00:07:49,103
- White House.

193
00:07:49,137 --> 00:07:50,505
- [Crow] Tell him I'm smoking.

194
00:07:51,372 --> 00:07:52,440
- No, not now.

195
00:07:54,775 --> 00:07:55,943
No.

196
00:07:55,977 --> 00:07:57,212
- [Joel] No self
respecting scientist

197
00:07:57,245 --> 00:07:58,278
would have his shirt
unbuttoned that far.

198
00:07:58,312 --> 00:07:59,513
I'm sorry.

199
00:07:59,547 --> 00:08:00,682
- Mr. Curan feels
certain that there's

200
00:08:01,915 --> 00:08:03,350
no hope for Captain Lockhart.

201
00:08:05,687 --> 00:08:07,188
Yes, sir.

202
00:08:07,222 --> 00:08:08,522
- [Tom] Plus he's smoking.

203
00:08:10,558 --> 00:08:12,527
- [Joel] Isn't that
a David Bowie song?

204
00:08:14,962 --> 00:08:16,997
- [Crow] I hate cappuccino.

205
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:18,665
- [Announcer] One
minute, 30 seconds.

206
00:08:19,833 --> 00:08:22,470
- [Tom] Hey, scientists
shouldn't litter.

207
00:08:29,243 --> 00:08:30,477
- You all right?

208
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:32,112
- [Tom] Shut up and
throw the cup away.

209
00:08:34,248 --> 00:08:35,282
- [Announcer] One minute.

210
00:08:35,315 --> 00:08:36,483
- How's our boss holding up?

211
00:08:36,517 --> 00:08:38,352
- [Tom] He's probably smoking.

212
00:08:38,385 --> 00:08:40,453
- As usual he's not said a word.

213
00:08:40,487 --> 00:08:42,188
- I wouldn't get too close
to him if I were you.

214
00:08:42,223 --> 00:08:44,158
- [Crow] Yeah, his breath
could stop a Titan missile.

215
00:08:44,192 --> 00:08:46,927
- I was figuring on
giving him a sedative.

216
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:47,795
- [Tom] Funny.

217
00:08:49,930 --> 00:08:52,300
- [Joel] Hey, they all
got stripes in their hair.

218
00:08:52,333 --> 00:08:55,168
- [Crow] Yeah, it looks like
land of the skunk people.

219
00:08:55,202 --> 00:08:58,038
- [Tom] Hair styles
by Gordon of Gotham.

220
00:08:58,072 --> 00:08:59,940
- Will be in about 60 seconds.

221
00:09:01,108 --> 00:09:01,942
Well,

222
00:09:02,810 --> 00:09:05,012
we've done it your way twice.

223
00:09:05,045 --> 00:09:08,048
- Well now, we don't pretend
to know all the answers.

224
00:09:08,082 --> 00:09:09,750
- Apparently no one does.

225
00:09:09,783 --> 00:09:10,918
- [Tom] Except for her.

226
00:09:12,085 --> 00:09:13,420
- For every frontier
that's been explored.

227
00:09:13,453 --> 00:09:15,022
- [Announcer] 45 seconds.
- Men have died.

228
00:09:15,055 --> 00:09:17,858
We've only lost two men on
the strangest frontier of all.

229
00:09:17,892 --> 00:09:19,393
- [Tom] Cable.

230
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:21,362
- There are millions of people
in this world, Dr. Weitzberg,

231
00:09:21,395 --> 00:09:23,130
but there are only four men left

232
00:09:23,163 --> 00:09:25,298
who can handle the lunar rocket.

233
00:09:25,332 --> 00:09:27,468
- [Joel] The Beatles?
- [Tom] And her.

234
00:09:27,502 --> 00:09:30,037
- We've lost one-third
of the men we have.

235
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:32,205
It's like a conveyor
to oblivion.

236
00:09:32,238 --> 00:09:33,473
- [Crow] Like your career.

237
00:09:33,507 --> 00:09:34,875
- [Announcer] 30 seconds.

238
00:09:34,908 --> 00:09:37,243
- The situation that happened
to those first two men

239
00:09:37,278 --> 00:09:39,847
was not only duplicated,
but widely exaggerated

240
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:41,048
in their training.

241
00:09:41,082 --> 00:09:42,817
- Those men did not
have a mental crack up.

242
00:09:42,850 --> 00:09:44,318
I'd stake my life on it.

243
00:09:44,352 --> 00:09:47,288
- There is no duplicate
for the solitude of space.

244
00:09:47,322 --> 00:09:50,057
If those men could've looked
in their viewing screen

245
00:09:50,090 --> 00:09:51,925
and seen two, three,
or more sister ships

246
00:09:51,958 --> 00:09:53,427
alongside out there.
- 15 seconds.

247
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:54,395
- They wouldn't have panicked!

248
00:09:54,428 --> 00:09:55,195
- [Tom] Bang.
- [Crow] Ow.

249
00:09:55,228 --> 00:09:56,897
- You heard those tapes.

250
00:09:56,930 --> 00:09:58,532
Those men did not panic!

251
00:09:58,565 --> 00:09:59,400
- [Crow] Why are they shouting?

252
00:09:59,433 --> 00:10:01,402
- Nine, eight

253
00:10:01,435 --> 00:10:02,670
seven.
- The remaining four men.

254
00:10:02,703 --> 00:10:04,105
- [Tom] Seven seconds
till he slaps the table.

255
00:10:04,138 --> 00:10:06,073
- [Announcer] Five, four.
- Do it without me.

256
00:10:06,107 --> 00:10:09,410
- [Tom] Four, three, two, one.

257
00:10:09,443 --> 00:10:10,543
- [Announcer] Zero.

258
00:10:10,577 --> 00:10:11,812
Oxygen depleted.

259
00:10:11,845 --> 00:10:12,980
- [Tom] Slap table.
(slapping table)

260
00:10:13,013 --> 00:10:13,847
Thank you.

261
00:10:15,015 --> 00:10:16,950
- I'll see you in
Washington, tomorrow.

262
00:10:18,385 --> 00:10:20,420
- [Tom] I'm going
to get some air.

263
00:10:20,453 --> 00:10:21,888
(Donna crying)

264
00:10:21,922 --> 00:10:23,390
- [Crow] Hey, she's making
that loon call with her hands.

265
00:10:23,423 --> 00:10:26,160
(Crow whistling)

266
00:10:26,193 --> 00:10:27,728
- [Joel] Cool, Steve,
did you ever think about

267
00:10:27,762 --> 00:10:29,063
getting your hair frosted?

268
00:10:30,363 --> 00:10:31,532
- Steve.

269
00:10:31,565 --> 00:10:32,767
- [Joel] I don't you
have a widow's peak now.

270
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,835
- Think he knew about
Donna and Mel Lockhart?

271
00:10:36,237 --> 00:10:37,203
- I don't know, Doc.

272
00:10:38,805 --> 00:10:41,008
If he did, I doubt it
would make much difference.

273
00:10:41,042 --> 00:10:43,443
- [Tom] Let's see, you
mess with this switch,

274
00:10:43,477 --> 00:10:47,013
twist that, flip this.

275
00:10:47,047 --> 00:10:48,248
Oh, someone's coming.

276
00:10:48,282 --> 00:10:49,850
Good luck with
that. (snickering)

277
00:10:49,883 --> 00:10:52,653
- Well, Steve, might as well
write our formal (mumbling).

278
00:10:54,355 --> 00:10:56,623
- I can't take much
more of this, Doc.

279
00:10:56,657 --> 00:10:57,592
- [Crow] Yeah, soon
I'll have to move off

280
00:10:57,625 --> 00:10:58,925
of the corner of this formica.

281
00:10:58,958 --> 00:11:00,393
- No he can justify it.

282
00:11:00,427 --> 00:11:02,930
- Oh, our boss is Secretary
of Space Operation,

283
00:11:02,963 --> 00:11:05,132
and that cabinet
post entitles him

284
00:11:05,165 --> 00:11:06,800
to be a very daring man.

285
00:11:06,833 --> 00:11:09,137
- No, we got to stop him, Doc.

286
00:11:09,170 --> 00:11:10,705
- All right, Steve, before
you go see him tomorrow

287
00:11:10,738 --> 00:11:11,938
stop by my office.

288
00:11:11,972 --> 00:11:14,342
- [Joel] I'll give
you a protein rinse.

289
00:11:14,375 --> 00:11:16,177
- I don't get it.

290
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:18,012
I just don't get it!

291
00:11:18,045 --> 00:11:19,747
The pattern is the
same as before.

292
00:11:21,682 --> 00:11:25,018
Perfect takeoff, clear
contact all the way,

293
00:11:25,052 --> 00:11:27,020
perfect landing.

294
00:11:27,053 --> 00:11:29,657
You heard Lockhart when he
was on the lunar surface.

295
00:11:30,758 --> 00:11:33,127
Liftoff from the moon, perfect.

296
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:34,895
One hour later, nothing,

297
00:11:34,928 --> 00:11:36,630
and we had him right here, Doc.

298
00:11:36,663 --> 00:11:38,665
- [Tom] Sure wish I had
that command of my arms.

299
00:11:38,698 --> 00:11:39,900
- If he wanted to talk to us,

300
00:11:39,933 --> 00:11:41,735
he didn't have to
push a lousy button.

301
00:11:41,768 --> 00:11:42,703
- [Tom] Look at
that fluid motion.

302
00:11:42,737 --> 00:11:43,537
- [Lockhart] Do you read me?

303
00:11:43,570 --> 00:11:44,772
Do you read me?

304
00:11:44,805 --> 00:11:48,742
Lockhart, X20,
approaching ionosphere.

305
00:11:54,982 --> 00:11:55,917
- [Tom] Hey, someone
screwed up all the knobs.

306
00:11:55,950 --> 00:11:58,352
- Someone please come in.

307
00:11:58,385 --> 00:12:00,320
- [Joel] Max Hedroom's dad.

308
00:12:00,353 --> 00:12:03,223
(machine whirring)

309
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,695
- [Joel] And the music
goes round and round

310
00:12:07,728 --> 00:12:09,230
and it comes out here.

311
00:12:09,263 --> 00:12:11,165
- [Crow] Hey, it's a spirograph.

312
00:12:11,198 --> 00:12:12,098
- X20 to moon lab.

313
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,002
Do you read me?

314
00:12:16,537 --> 00:12:19,607
- Lockhart, this is moon lab.

315
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:21,875
Curan, Steve Curan.

316
00:12:21,908 --> 00:12:22,910
Come in X20.

317
00:12:23,810 --> 00:12:25,045
- Steve.

318
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:25,980
- [Tom] Look, it's
Tammy Faye Bakker.

319
00:12:26,013 --> 00:12:27,213
- It's coming on again.

320
00:12:27,247 --> 00:12:27,948
- [Joel] Yeah, he looks
more like Robert Smith

321
00:12:27,982 --> 00:12:28,782
from The Cure.

322
00:12:28,815 --> 00:12:31,118
- My god, my god,

323
00:12:33,087 --> 00:12:33,987
Kill, kill!

324
00:12:35,155 --> 00:12:37,090
- [Crow] Looks like
he needs the cure.

325
00:12:37,123 --> 00:12:38,158
- What's wrong with that man?

326
00:12:38,192 --> 00:12:40,160
- I don't know, Steve.

327
00:12:40,193 --> 00:12:41,962
This is Doc Weitzberg.

328
00:12:41,995 --> 00:12:43,830
How long have you
been without oxygen?

329
00:12:43,863 --> 00:12:46,800
- 15, maybe 20 minutes.

330
00:12:47,735 --> 00:12:48,702
- That's impossible.

331
00:12:49,937 --> 00:12:51,605
- Why did you break contact?

332
00:12:51,638 --> 00:12:54,442
- [Lockhart] (mumbling)
contact, please.

333
00:12:54,475 --> 00:12:57,478
Kill, no, no, don't.

334
00:12:58,645 --> 00:13:00,915
Steve, I can't make it.

335
00:13:02,148 --> 00:13:03,483
Lost control.

336
00:13:03,517 --> 00:13:05,218
- [Tom] Have you considered
frosting your hair?

337
00:13:05,252 --> 00:13:07,353
- [Lockhart] Push the red.

338
00:13:07,387 --> 00:13:09,122
- He can't push that red
button, it'll blow him up.

339
00:13:09,157 --> 00:13:12,158
- [Tom] Push the red button.

340
00:13:12,192 --> 00:13:13,393
- I can't make it.

341
00:13:13,427 --> 00:13:14,195
- [Crow] I think he's
taken acting lessons

342
00:13:14,228 --> 00:13:15,128
from William Shatner.

343
00:13:16,330 --> 00:13:18,398
- [Lockhart] Something
makes my arm move.

344
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:20,600
Makes me do things.

345
00:13:20,633 --> 00:13:21,468
Kill, kill!

346
00:13:22,937 --> 00:13:27,942
- [Tom] This is Major
Tom to Ground Control.

347
00:13:28,808 --> 00:13:29,243
- [Lockhart] I'm 200000 feet.

348
00:13:30,343 --> 00:13:31,312
Push the red.

349
00:13:31,345 --> 00:13:32,747
Something end.

350
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:33,813
Push the red.

351
00:13:33,847 --> 00:13:35,615
- Why do you want us to push it?

352
00:13:35,648 --> 00:13:37,050
You can push it if you want to.

353
00:13:37,083 --> 00:13:39,187
- [Joel] It's better
when you push it.

354
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:40,922
- [Lockhart] I
can't move my arms.

355
00:13:40,955 --> 00:13:41,888
Kill, kill!

356
00:13:44,658 --> 00:13:47,127
- [Tom] Looks like the
DOW's up about three points.

357
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:48,528
- Push the red.

358
00:13:48,562 --> 00:13:49,830
No more time.

359
00:13:50,932 --> 00:13:51,798
- What's his position?

360
00:13:51,832 --> 00:13:52,833
Where is he?

361
00:13:54,602 --> 00:13:55,402
- Inland.

362
00:13:57,337 --> 00:13:59,072
Populated area of California.

363
00:13:59,105 --> 00:14:00,707
- 140000.

364
00:14:00,740 --> 00:14:02,142
Push the red!

365
00:14:02,175 --> 00:14:04,245
- [Tom] Must push button.

366
00:14:07,413 --> 00:14:08,848
- My god, my god.

367
00:14:09,950 --> 00:14:12,452
God, Steve, help me, help me!

368
00:14:12,485 --> 00:14:13,753
- [Tom] Well he
still has good teeth.

369
00:14:13,787 --> 00:14:15,455
- [Lockhart] The red.

370
00:14:15,488 --> 00:14:16,290
- [Joel] Here, I'll push it.

371
00:14:16,323 --> 00:14:17,323
Let me be the one.

372
00:14:17,357 --> 00:14:19,327
I want to push this thing.

373
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:20,193
- 70000.

374
00:14:21,295 --> 00:14:22,495
Push it!

375
00:14:22,528 --> 00:14:25,065
- [Crow] Push that
and he's an astro-not.

376
00:14:25,098 --> 00:14:26,400
(Lockhart screaming)
(spaceship exploding)

377
00:14:26,433 --> 00:14:29,035
(upbeat music)

378
00:14:34,642 --> 00:14:35,442
- [Lockhart] My god.

379
00:14:35,475 --> 00:14:37,210
God, Steve, help me.

380
00:14:37,243 --> 00:14:38,912
Help me!

381
00:14:38,945 --> 00:14:40,113
Push the red.

382
00:14:40,147 --> 00:14:41,348
70000.

383
00:14:41,382 --> 00:14:42,717
- [Joel] Sounds like
my old Phy Ed teacher,

384
00:14:42,750 --> 00:14:43,550
push it.

385
00:14:43,583 --> 00:14:44,752
Push it.

386
00:14:44,785 --> 00:14:47,120
- [Lockhart] Push it!

387
00:14:47,153 --> 00:14:48,888
- [Crow] And then
the tape ran out.

388
00:14:49,857 --> 00:14:51,425
But we've got it on CD too.

389
00:14:51,458 --> 00:14:54,895
- Well, that's one tape we
don't want Donald to transcribe.

390
00:14:54,928 --> 00:14:56,430
- Don't worry, I'll
take care of it.

391
00:14:56,463 --> 00:14:57,932
- [Tom] It's pretty morbid
to keep listening to that.

392
00:14:57,965 --> 00:15:00,467
- How can a man continue
to function without oxygen

393
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:01,268
for 20 minutes?

394
00:15:01,302 --> 00:15:02,435
- He can't.

395
00:15:03,603 --> 00:15:04,505
- Lockhart did.

396
00:15:05,672 --> 00:15:09,008
So we know something
kept him functioning.

397
00:15:09,042 --> 00:15:10,910
- A substitute for oxygen?

398
00:15:10,945 --> 00:15:12,145
- [Tom] New oxygen light.

399
00:15:12,178 --> 00:15:13,247
- I don't know Steve.

400
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:15,315
But ever since we lost Nelson,

401
00:15:15,348 --> 00:15:19,185
an idea's been buzzing around
in my brain like a fly.

402
00:15:19,218 --> 00:15:22,188
A very persistent fly.
(Crow buzzing)

403
00:15:22,222 --> 00:15:25,758
- After hearing Lockhart
the buzzing stops.

404
00:15:25,792 --> 00:15:27,593
You know, I think
I've hit on something.

405
00:15:27,628 --> 00:15:29,028
Want it hear it out then?

406
00:15:29,062 --> 00:15:29,863
- Go ahead.

407
00:15:29,897 --> 00:15:31,132
Have at it.

408
00:15:31,165 --> 00:15:34,200
- Well I think what kept
Lockhart going was life.

409
00:15:35,568 --> 00:15:37,638
An independent, vital force.

410
00:15:37,672 --> 00:15:39,440
Something living.

411
00:15:39,473 --> 00:15:40,707
- [Crow] Oh, and I suppose
next you'll tell me

412
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:42,475
there's rings around Uranus.

413
00:15:42,508 --> 00:15:45,378
- Well living covers a
lot of territory, Steve.

414
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:49,683
And I don't think Lockhart
was alone on his return flight

415
00:15:49,717 --> 00:15:50,683
anymore than Nelson was.

416
00:15:50,717 --> 00:15:52,085
- Well now.

417
00:15:52,118 --> 00:15:54,287
- (laughing) I'm not
talking about the three men.

418
00:15:54,322 --> 00:15:56,057
As a matter of fact,
I may not talk at all,

419
00:15:56,090 --> 00:15:57,992
unless I get some encouragement.

420
00:15:58,025 --> 00:15:58,825
- Well, what do you need?

421
00:15:58,858 --> 00:16:00,460
A drink maybe?

422
00:16:00,493 --> 00:16:01,928
- [Joel] What, are
you coming on to me?

423
00:16:01,962 --> 00:16:03,930
- And you're the one
that can get Berringer

424
00:16:03,963 --> 00:16:05,298
to give it to me.

425
00:16:05,332 --> 00:16:06,800
- Time?
For what?

426
00:16:06,833 --> 00:16:09,770
- Time to prove that this
force, this new life,

427
00:16:09,803 --> 00:16:11,205
was born on the X20.

428
00:16:12,172 --> 00:16:13,273
- Go ahead.

429
00:16:14,675 --> 00:16:16,777
- I need you with me
all the way, Steve.

430
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:18,045
- [Crow] He asked
me, he asked me.

431
00:16:18,078 --> 00:16:18,912
- I am with you.

432
00:16:21,582 --> 00:16:22,750
- What makes life?

433
00:16:22,783 --> 00:16:25,918
A certain particular
combination of molecules

434
00:16:25,952 --> 00:16:28,822
or carbon or
silicons or nitrogen

435
00:16:28,855 --> 00:16:30,723
or any number of these things.

436
00:16:30,757 --> 00:16:31,758
- [Tom] Blah, blah, blah.

437
00:16:31,792 --> 00:16:33,527
- Certain temperature.

438
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:34,428
Mix well.

439
00:16:34,462 --> 00:16:35,928
Call it what you will.

440
00:16:35,962 --> 00:16:37,130
- [Tom] Serve over cracked ice
and garnish with a parasol.

441
00:16:37,163 --> 00:16:39,800
- Swirling together
in tiny orbits,

442
00:16:39,833 --> 00:16:43,703
fuses these things into a
precise energy relationship,

443
00:16:43,737 --> 00:16:45,472
which we call life.

444
00:16:45,505 --> 00:16:46,507
- No, wait a minute.

445
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:49,008
What you're saying is that.

446
00:16:50,143 --> 00:16:51,912
- [Joel] Boy, he's slow, huh?

447
00:16:51,945 --> 00:16:53,480
- Life might mutate,

448
00:16:53,513 --> 00:16:56,050
or even fully evolve
in a matter of hours

449
00:16:56,083 --> 00:16:57,683
or even minutes.

450
00:16:57,718 --> 00:16:58,918
- [Tom] No, you're wrong.

451
00:16:58,952 --> 00:17:00,387
- What happens when we
send a man up there?

452
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:01,188
- [Joel] Speed it up.

453
00:17:01,222 --> 00:17:02,422
Let's go.

454
00:17:02,455 --> 00:17:04,425
- We don't just send
a man in a rocket.

455
00:17:04,458 --> 00:17:05,525
We send up living cells.

456
00:17:05,558 --> 00:17:06,527
- [Tom] They don't (mumbling).

457
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:07,360
- [Joel] Snap out of it.

458
00:17:07,393 --> 00:17:08,562
- Germs.

459
00:17:08,595 --> 00:17:10,397
We throw in
radioactive (mumbling).

460
00:17:10,430 --> 00:17:13,667
We introduce all
these to cosmic rays.

461
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:15,168
Do we upset this balance?

462
00:17:15,202 --> 00:17:17,103
Do we start a cycle?

463
00:17:17,137 --> 00:17:20,173
Does a living cell from
Earth romance a cosmic ray

464
00:17:20,207 --> 00:17:22,175
and give birth to an
illegitimate monster,

465
00:17:22,208 --> 00:17:23,910
who makes his nest in?

466
00:17:23,943 --> 00:17:25,045
- [Tom] I wonder if
there's an abridged version

467
00:17:25,078 --> 00:17:26,213
of this movie.

468
00:17:26,247 --> 00:17:27,547
- [Doc] Beginning to (mumbling).

469
00:17:28,482 --> 00:17:29,717
Perhaps-

470
00:17:29,750 --> 00:17:30,350
- [Crow] If there
is, let's burn it.

471
00:17:30,383 --> 00:17:31,618
- Be dying.

472
00:17:31,652 --> 00:17:32,820
Anything evolving
so rapidly could.

473
00:17:32,853 --> 00:17:34,455
- [Tom] Never burn your bridges.

474
00:17:34,488 --> 00:17:35,322
- Well Steve.

475
00:17:37,423 --> 00:17:38,925
- Quite a chunk.

476
00:17:38,958 --> 00:17:40,827
- Oh, throw out the logic
pills when you swallow it.

477
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:43,763
This theory can't be measured
by anything we already know.

478
00:17:44,798 --> 00:17:45,965
(Tom singing)

479
00:17:45,998 --> 00:17:47,233
- Sounds like a good case.

480
00:17:47,267 --> 00:17:49,268
- I was hoping you'd say that.

481
00:17:49,302 --> 00:17:50,670
- Berringer won't.

482
00:17:50,703 --> 00:17:52,205
He won't go for a theory,
even if it's true.

483
00:17:52,238 --> 00:17:55,408
- Then lie to him,
but buy me the time.

484
00:17:57,110 --> 00:17:58,078
- [Crow] Buy me things.

485
00:17:58,112 --> 00:17:58,912
Buy me things.

486
00:17:58,945 --> 00:18:01,482
(upbeat music)

487
00:18:02,582 --> 00:18:03,483
- [Joel] All right.
- No dancing.

488
00:18:03,517 --> 00:18:04,852
No dancing, not allowed.

489
00:18:07,053 --> 00:18:08,155
No dancing.

490
00:18:08,188 --> 00:18:09,557
No dancing, not allowed.

491
00:18:09,590 --> 00:18:10,790
- [Tom] What a grouch.

492
00:18:10,823 --> 00:18:12,658
- [Joel] It's just
like Footloose.

493
00:18:12,692 --> 00:18:14,662
- No rats, not allowed.

494
00:18:14,695 --> 00:18:16,163
- [Tom] Unless
they're on the menu.

495
00:18:17,832 --> 00:18:18,998
No acting, not allowed.

496
00:18:20,433 --> 00:18:22,502
- And you were gonna
be a nurse, Patsy?

497
00:18:23,637 --> 00:18:25,338
- Well what are you
doing with them?

498
00:18:25,372 --> 00:18:27,908
- I picked them up at the
Express office for Grandpa.

499
00:18:27,942 --> 00:18:29,543
They're for the lab at school.

500
00:18:29,577 --> 00:18:31,612
- Well, maybe you're
grandfather's a
biology professor,

501
00:18:31,645 --> 00:18:34,447
and you're boyfriend's
a premed student,

502
00:18:34,482 --> 00:18:38,152
but my father's a sheriff, and
rats and the law don't mix.

503
00:18:38,185 --> 00:18:39,687
- [Joel] I thought
they were synonymous.

504
00:18:41,922 --> 00:18:42,755
- Voila.

505
00:18:43,623 --> 00:18:44,858
No rats.

506
00:18:44,892 --> 00:18:45,692
- [Tom] Good trick.

507
00:18:45,725 --> 00:18:47,193
- You didn't bite.

508
00:18:47,227 --> 00:18:48,828
(woman grunting)

509
00:18:48,862 --> 00:18:49,963
When I said boyfriend.

510
00:18:51,865 --> 00:18:53,233
- You mean Paul?

511
00:18:53,267 --> 00:18:55,135
- Oh, then he isn't
just a mutual interest

512
00:18:55,168 --> 00:18:56,537
in hives and chicken pox.

513
00:18:57,838 --> 00:18:59,740
- He's very nice.

514
00:18:59,773 --> 00:19:01,007
- But how did you hook him?

515
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:03,610
- [Crow] Rubber
frog and pork rind.

516
00:19:03,643 --> 00:19:05,112
- The lone wolf they call us.

517
00:19:05,145 --> 00:19:06,347
(Crow howling)

518
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:07,848
Oh, I'm not saying
you're not his type,

519
00:19:07,882 --> 00:19:10,150
but he's just not with it.

520
00:19:10,183 --> 00:19:11,585
- Just good friends.

521
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:15,388
- [Crow] Joel, move your head.

522
00:19:15,422 --> 00:19:16,257
Thank you.

523
00:19:19,425 --> 00:19:20,693
- [Joel] Yeah, looks like bud,

524
00:19:20,727 --> 00:19:22,595
from My Three Sons.

525
00:19:24,130 --> 00:19:25,332
- You think I'm kidding, huh?

526
00:19:25,365 --> 00:19:26,767
You two see that
thing last night?

527
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:28,435
- [Joel] What, you
mean My Three Sons?

528
00:19:28,468 --> 00:19:29,737
- I didn't see anything.

529
00:19:29,770 --> 00:19:31,472
- Well, I phoned your father.

530
00:19:31,505 --> 00:19:34,340
Why there was a fireball in
the sky as big as a house.

531
00:19:34,373 --> 00:19:36,175
He didn't see it either.

532
00:19:36,210 --> 00:19:38,378
- Well, what do
you think it was?

533
00:19:38,412 --> 00:19:39,980
- Didn't I tell ya?

534
00:19:40,013 --> 00:19:41,815
Eat, drink, and be merry,

535
00:19:41,848 --> 00:19:43,017
tomorrow ya die.

536
00:19:43,050 --> 00:19:44,183
- [Tom] Well, no,
wouldn't it be today,

537
00:19:44,217 --> 00:19:44,852
if there was a fire
ball last night?

538
00:19:44,885 --> 00:19:46,553
(women laughing)

539
00:19:46,587 --> 00:19:48,188
- [Announcer] And now for
your local news, here in.

540
00:19:48,222 --> 00:19:49,690
- [Crow] Hey, he's got a woody.

541
00:19:49,723 --> 00:19:51,825
- [Announcer] The traffic
commission has decided.

542
00:20:02,768 --> 00:20:03,570
- Hi.

543
00:20:03,637 --> 00:20:04,938
- [Both] Hi Paul.

544
00:20:04,938 --> 00:20:07,040
- Come on, sit down beside me.

545
00:20:07,073 --> 00:20:09,542
- [Crow] Nah, I'll
sit where the rat was.

546
00:20:11,845 --> 00:20:12,678
- Come on, finish your milk.

547
00:20:12,745 --> 00:20:14,147
Let's get going.

548
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:15,348
- Can we stop by my house?

549
00:20:15,348 --> 00:20:17,183
- What for?
It's getting late.

550
00:20:17,250 --> 00:20:19,552
- I got to pick up
my bathing suit.

551
00:20:19,552 --> 00:20:20,987
- But I told you
I'd meet you here.

552
00:20:21,053 --> 00:20:22,555
You said you'd have
your suit with you.

553
00:20:22,588 --> 00:20:24,057
- [Tom] Mr. Charm.

554
00:20:24,057 --> 00:20:26,860
- I just can't get used to going
swimming at Christmas time.

555
00:20:26,893 --> 00:20:28,162
- [Crow] With a suit on?

556
00:20:28,162 --> 00:20:29,663
- [Patsy] Well I can
tell you one thing.

557
00:20:30,797 --> 00:20:31,998
- What's that?

558
00:20:32,065 --> 00:20:33,167
- It's for real.

559
00:20:33,167 --> 00:20:35,068
You sound like a
married couple already.

560
00:20:35,068 --> 00:20:36,670
(Paul and Marta laughing)

561
00:20:36,670 --> 00:20:37,637
- Come on, finish your milk.

562
00:20:37,670 --> 00:20:38,838
- [Joel] That's not milk.

563
00:20:38,872 --> 00:20:40,940
That's pancake batter
she's drinking.

564
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:42,542
- [Crow] Pancake batter?

565
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:43,577
You're soaking in it.

566
00:20:45,778 --> 00:20:47,380
- [Joel] Shotgun.
- Okay?

567
00:20:49,115 --> 00:20:51,918
I've got to drop these at
home for Grandpa anyhow.

568
00:20:51,952 --> 00:20:53,320
- Okay, let's get a move on.

569
00:20:53,353 --> 00:20:54,520
- [Crow] Hey, they
packed a lunch.

570
00:20:54,553 --> 00:20:55,555
- Bye Patsy.

571
00:20:55,588 --> 00:20:57,357
- Goodbye you two love birds.

572
00:20:57,390 --> 00:20:58,358
- [Tom] Ratatouille.

573
00:20:59,325 --> 00:21:00,593
- Hey Paul.

574
00:21:00,627 --> 00:21:01,862
What are they saying
over at the school

575
00:21:01,895 --> 00:21:03,330
about that thing in
the sky last night?

576
00:21:03,363 --> 00:21:04,765
- Well nothing, why?

577
00:21:04,798 --> 00:21:06,767
Everybody said it was a meteor.

578
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:08,368
(Soda shop owner laughing)

579
00:21:08,402 --> 00:21:09,837
- Hey, come on.

580
00:21:09,870 --> 00:21:12,638
Don't tell me you're still
scared of them flying saucers?

581
00:21:15,375 --> 00:21:18,512
- [Joel] Come on, bub, you
can't let fear run your life.

582
00:21:18,545 --> 00:21:21,615
- [Crow] Yeah, pops.
- Eat, drink, and be merry.

583
00:21:23,250 --> 00:21:24,652
- [Tom] Get over it.
(upbeat music)

584
00:21:24,685 --> 00:21:26,118
- [Crow] Yeah, take a
lesson from these teeners.

585
00:21:26,152 --> 00:21:27,787
Twist your troubles away.

586
00:21:35,228 --> 00:21:37,697
- [Tom] Let's twist again,
like we did last summer, Joel.

587
00:21:37,730 --> 00:21:38,898
- [Joel] Yeah.

588
00:21:39,867 --> 00:21:42,302
(tires screeching)

589
00:21:42,335 --> 00:21:43,603
- [Tom] Yowser.

590
00:21:43,637 --> 00:21:45,072
- [Joel] Look,
it's Ed Begley Sr.

591
00:21:45,105 --> 00:21:46,072
- Oh, hi children.

592
00:21:47,173 --> 00:21:47,973
- Hi.

593
00:21:50,210 --> 00:21:51,912
I picked this up for you
at the Express office.

594
00:21:51,945 --> 00:21:53,047
- Thank you.

595
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:54,748
- Love you, Grandpa.
Gotta run.

596
00:21:54,782 --> 00:21:56,950
- Hello there, Mr. Farnstrom.

597
00:21:56,983 --> 00:21:58,885
- I never really
understand how you do it.

598
00:21:58,918 --> 00:22:00,153
- [Tom] It's easy.

599
00:22:00,187 --> 00:22:01,555
I just tie the sleeves
around my neck.

600
00:22:01,588 --> 00:22:04,623
- It's 15 minutes
past the last bell,

601
00:22:04,657 --> 00:22:07,827
and already you are off
somewhere with my granddaughter.

602
00:22:08,895 --> 00:22:11,030
- Well, we have a system sir.

603
00:22:11,063 --> 00:22:12,432
A secret system.

604
00:22:12,465 --> 00:22:13,933
- Ah, secret.

605
00:22:13,967 --> 00:22:15,502
- [Crow] He looks
like a cross between

606
00:22:15,535 --> 00:22:17,337
Jerry Mathers and James Dean.

607
00:22:17,370 --> 00:22:19,172
- [Tom] Beaver without a cause.

608
00:22:19,205 --> 00:22:20,373
- (mumbling) that
you have to fail.

609
00:22:20,407 --> 00:22:22,108
Do work together.

610
00:22:22,142 --> 00:22:23,843
- [Tom] Joel, we got
to get out of here.

611
00:22:23,877 --> 00:22:24,677
Carry me.

612
00:22:24,712 --> 00:22:26,245
- Not me Professor.

613
00:22:26,278 --> 00:22:28,515
When I make it, I'll
make it on my own.

614
00:22:28,548 --> 00:22:29,750
All the way.

615
00:22:29,783 --> 00:22:30,750
- [Crow] Oop, sorry.

616
00:22:31,652 --> 00:22:32,818
I'm coming.

617
00:22:32,852 --> 00:22:35,955
- What did I tell you?
(mysterious music)

618
00:22:52,072 --> 00:22:52,872
(bowling pins falling)

619
00:22:52,905 --> 00:22:54,273
- Hey, baby splits.

620
00:22:54,307 --> 00:22:56,510
Wow, that's gonna
be tough to beat.

621
00:22:56,543 --> 00:22:58,178
- [Tom] Here we are
in the fifth frame.

622
00:22:58,212 --> 00:23:01,648
Crow is staring at a
nasty six-ten split.

623
00:23:01,682 --> 00:23:02,915
He really needs this one.

624
00:23:04,752 --> 00:23:06,687
Oh, good hook, yes.

625
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:07,853
(pins falling)
(Crow yelling)

626
00:23:07,887 --> 00:23:08,755
- All right.
- Bingo!

627
00:23:08,788 --> 00:23:10,123
- Great shot.

628
00:23:10,157 --> 00:23:11,357
- Nice shot Crow.

629
00:23:11,390 --> 00:23:12,825
- Hey, that puts
me over 120, Joel.

630
00:23:12,858 --> 00:23:14,060
- All right, that was excellent!

631
00:23:14,093 --> 00:23:15,028
- Ow, great game.
- These new arms

632
00:23:15,062 --> 00:23:16,262
are working great.

633
00:23:16,295 --> 00:23:17,730
- You know, but I hate
these rental shoes,

634
00:23:17,763 --> 00:23:20,400
'cause no matter how much of
that stuff they spray into 'em,

635
00:23:20,433 --> 00:23:22,168
your feet still smell funny.

636
00:23:22,202 --> 00:23:24,805
- Smell, I didn't
know you could smell.

637
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:27,607
- Did I say smell?

638
00:23:27,640 --> 00:23:30,010
I meant spectral
analysis, of course.

639
00:23:30,043 --> 00:23:32,012
- Hey, I was thinking,
let's play murderball now.

640
00:23:32,045 --> 00:23:32,845
What do you say?

641
00:23:32,878 --> 00:23:33,713
- Uh, no.

642
00:23:34,647 --> 00:23:35,615
- Wait a minute.

643
00:23:35,648 --> 00:23:36,983
I created you guys,

644
00:23:37,017 --> 00:23:39,318
so when I say we play
murderball, we play murderball.

645
00:23:39,352 --> 00:23:41,253
- Okay, not it.
- Not it.

646
00:23:41,287 --> 00:23:42,288
- Let's do it over.

647
00:23:42,322 --> 00:23:43,557
- Not it.
- Not it.

648
00:23:46,092 --> 00:23:47,860
- Well how about rock,
paper, scissors, huh?

649
00:23:47,893 --> 00:23:50,163
- Oh, no way, I'm not
playing that with you again.

650
00:23:50,197 --> 00:23:51,063
Look at my hands.

651
00:23:51,097 --> 00:23:52,532
Look, see.

652
00:23:52,565 --> 00:23:54,600
You know, every time we
play, I got to be scissors,

653
00:23:54,633 --> 00:23:55,835
and you pick rock all the time.

654
00:23:55,868 --> 00:23:57,803
- Hey, it's just the
luck of the draw, Crow.

655
00:23:57,837 --> 00:23:59,072
Come on.

656
00:23:59,105 --> 00:24:01,708
- Okay, Joel, you
win everything.

657
00:24:01,742 --> 00:24:03,243
- Yeah, bye, see ya.

658
00:24:03,277 --> 00:24:04,978
- See ya later, Joel.

659
00:24:06,378 --> 00:24:07,813
- Go home to your mom, yeah.

660
00:24:07,847 --> 00:24:08,848
We're your friends.

661
00:24:08,882 --> 00:24:10,250
- [Tom and Crow] Not.

662
00:24:10,283 --> 00:24:11,585
- Come on, let's ditch him.

663
00:24:12,618 --> 00:24:13,820
- Oh that's great, I get it.

664
00:24:13,853 --> 00:24:15,255
Yeah, yeah, thanks Joel.

665
00:24:15,288 --> 00:24:17,357
Thanks for emptying my load pan.

666
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:19,592
Thanks for creating us, Joel.

667
00:24:19,625 --> 00:24:22,862
Thanks for making us
intrinsically human, Joel.

668
00:24:22,895 --> 00:24:25,165
Hey Cambot, come
in a little bit.

669
00:24:25,198 --> 00:24:26,933
Cambot, I want you to
remind me of something.

670
00:24:26,967 --> 00:24:30,037
Next time I make a
robot, no more free will.

671
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:31,203
That's it.

672
00:24:31,237 --> 00:24:32,472
We got commercial.

673
00:24:33,407 --> 00:24:34,707
I hate this.

674
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:37,310
(upbeat music)

675
00:24:41,313 --> 00:24:44,050
(peaceful music)

676
00:24:45,752 --> 00:24:47,687
(Marta giggling)

677
00:24:47,720 --> 00:24:48,688
- [Tom] Ow.

678
00:24:48,722 --> 00:24:49,622
- [Joel] Ouch, ooh, sting, pain.

679
00:24:49,655 --> 00:24:50,557
- [Tom] Oh, oh, hot, hot.

680
00:24:50,590 --> 00:24:51,992
Hot, hot, hot, hot, ow, thud.

681
00:24:52,025 --> 00:24:54,293
(man grunting)

682
00:24:54,327 --> 00:24:57,397
- (laughing) Are you hurt?

683
00:24:57,430 --> 00:24:59,298
- (grunting) Only my pride.

684
00:24:59,332 --> 00:25:00,367
- [Tom] And my lumbar.

685
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:01,167
- [Crow] He landed on it.

686
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:02,602
- Poor boy.

687
00:25:02,635 --> 00:25:05,905
The sensitive male
ego, so easily wounded.

688
00:25:06,807 --> 00:25:07,973
- I think I know now the secret

689
00:25:08,007 --> 00:25:10,377
of how to be successful
with a woman.

690
00:25:10,410 --> 00:25:11,510
- You do?

691
00:25:11,545 --> 00:25:12,978
Tell me.

692
00:25:13,012 --> 00:25:15,615
- You have to be weak, pathetic,

693
00:25:15,648 --> 00:25:16,917
then they feel sorry for you.

694
00:25:16,950 --> 00:25:18,918
- [Tom] You're all
those things, and less.

695
00:25:18,952 --> 00:25:21,387
- I think you discovered it.

696
00:25:21,420 --> 00:25:22,855
- I know I have.

697
00:25:22,888 --> 00:25:24,490
I always wondered why
some spindly little runts

698
00:25:24,523 --> 00:25:26,693
had the girls chasing after 'em.

699
00:25:26,727 --> 00:25:28,795
Because they felt sorry for 'em.

700
00:25:28,828 --> 00:25:30,063
- Of course.

701
00:25:30,097 --> 00:25:30,997
- [Tom] And usually
they have money too.

702
00:25:31,030 --> 00:25:34,200
- But you were fooling me.

703
00:25:34,233 --> 00:25:35,135
You're not weak.

704
00:25:36,970 --> 00:25:37,803
- Darn right.

705
00:25:38,905 --> 00:25:39,973
Now I've got you.

706
00:25:41,173 --> 00:25:43,510
- [Crow] Hey, is this
From Here to Eternity?

707
00:25:43,543 --> 00:25:46,045
- [Tom] No, it just
seems like an eternity.

708
00:25:46,078 --> 00:25:46,913
- Oh Paul.

709
00:25:48,448 --> 00:25:50,983
I guess I'm not like
those other women.

710
00:25:51,017 --> 00:25:52,552
I don't like weak men at all.

711
00:25:54,120 --> 00:25:55,955
- Listen, will you
once and for all

712
00:25:55,988 --> 00:25:57,590
give me a serious answer?

713
00:25:58,492 --> 00:25:59,692
- What about?

714
00:25:59,725 --> 00:26:01,393
- Are you going home, to Sweden,

715
00:26:01,427 --> 00:26:02,595
after the semester's over?

716
00:26:02,628 --> 00:26:04,063
- Why not?

717
00:26:04,097 --> 00:26:07,100
I only planned to come
to California for a year.

718
00:26:07,133 --> 00:26:08,335
To see Grandfather,

719
00:26:08,368 --> 00:26:09,835
and go to an American
school for a while.

720
00:26:09,868 --> 00:26:11,270
- [Tom] Meet some
football teams.

721
00:26:11,303 --> 00:26:13,907
- Nothing has come up
to change my plans.

722
00:26:13,940 --> 00:26:14,707
- What do you mean?

723
00:26:14,740 --> 00:26:16,208
What about me?

724
00:26:16,242 --> 00:26:17,677
- No.

725
00:26:17,710 --> 00:26:19,745
I planned there
would be a boy too.

726
00:26:19,778 --> 00:26:22,448
- [Joel] Oh, you little minx.

727
00:26:24,583 --> 00:26:26,853
- [Tom] Oh no, don't throw
my shorts in the sand.

728
00:26:26,887 --> 00:26:28,053
Damn you!

729
00:26:28,087 --> 00:26:31,223
(Marta laughing)

730
00:26:31,257 --> 00:26:34,360
All right, she's
a stripping minx.

731
00:26:34,393 --> 00:26:36,262
- Don't come any closer.

732
00:26:36,295 --> 00:26:37,597
I've nothing on.

733
00:26:37,630 --> 00:26:40,700
- Ah, faker, you haven't
had time to undress.

734
00:26:40,733 --> 00:26:41,968
- Maybe I didn't.

735
00:26:42,002 --> 00:26:43,068
- [Joel] How would he know?

736
00:26:43,102 --> 00:26:43,970
- But I have now.

737
00:26:45,605 --> 00:26:49,308
- Okay, you win.
- [Tom] I'll accept that.

738
00:26:50,710 --> 00:26:52,812
Soon to be naked is better
than not naked at all.

739
00:26:54,347 --> 00:26:57,817
- You know, I don't think your
grandfather approves of me.

740
00:26:57,850 --> 00:26:58,885
- [Joel] 'Cause you're naked.

741
00:26:58,918 --> 00:27:00,420
- You're wrong.

742
00:27:00,453 --> 00:27:03,490
He considers you the best
student in his class.

743
00:27:03,523 --> 00:27:05,392
- I meant as a
member of the family.

744
00:27:08,862 --> 00:27:10,897
What do I have to do,
say it in Swedish?

745
00:27:10,930 --> 00:27:13,165
- [Joel] How do you
say moon in Swedish?

746
00:27:14,033 --> 00:27:15,402
- [Tom] Hubba hubba.

747
00:27:15,435 --> 00:27:18,303
- Paul, what does it
mean, I'm stacked?

748
00:27:18,338 --> 00:27:19,438
And you're not worthy?

749
00:27:20,873 --> 00:27:22,075
- Stacked?

750
00:27:22,108 --> 00:27:23,943
- [Crow] Stacked means
you're really smart.

751
00:27:24,943 --> 00:27:27,347
(water splashing)

752
00:27:27,380 --> 00:27:29,382
- Patsy, this afternoon.

753
00:27:30,783 --> 00:27:33,387
- [Crow] Hey, why's she wearing
a swimsuit with a codpiece?

754
00:27:33,420 --> 00:27:35,688
- [Tom] Oh, just
for the halibut.

755
00:27:35,722 --> 00:27:36,990
- Is something wrong?
- [Tom] (laughing) Sorry.

756
00:27:37,023 --> 00:27:38,992
- [Crow] Stop it, you're
giving me a haddock.

757
00:27:39,025 --> 00:27:41,862
- [Tom] (laughing) You're
a pain in the bass.

758
00:27:41,895 --> 00:27:43,363
- [Crow] You have no sole.

759
00:27:43,397 --> 00:27:46,132
(Marta mumbling)

760
00:27:48,368 --> 00:27:50,670
- How do you say it in Swedish?

761
00:27:50,703 --> 00:27:51,537
- Say what?

762
00:27:52,572 --> 00:27:55,108
- Nothing, let's hit the water.

763
00:27:55,142 --> 00:27:56,977
- You know something?

764
00:27:57,010 --> 00:27:59,678
American men marry
much too young.

765
00:28:01,047 --> 00:28:02,815
- You know something.

766
00:28:02,848 --> 00:28:05,118
It's because of
Swedish girls like you.

767
00:28:05,152 --> 00:28:06,787
- [Tom] The kind that
get naked in public.

768
00:28:06,820 --> 00:28:08,955
- [Crow] Hey, last one in
the fjord's a rotten egg.

769
00:28:08,988 --> 00:28:10,823
(dramatic music)

770
00:28:10,857 --> 00:28:12,458
- [Joel] Hey, it's
a beach thermometer.

771
00:28:12,492 --> 00:28:13,693
- Well, Donna, I've
called everywhere

772
00:28:13,727 --> 00:28:15,195
and haven't been
able to find him.

773
00:28:15,228 --> 00:28:17,097
Didn't he say
where he was going?

774
00:28:17,130 --> 00:28:19,798
- No, he was quite
upset when he left.

775
00:28:19,832 --> 00:28:21,467
He banged on the
typewriter for a while.

776
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:22,735
He was in a mood.

777
00:28:22,768 --> 00:28:23,970
- I've got to talk
to him right away.

778
00:28:24,003 --> 00:28:24,903
It's urgent

779
00:28:24,937 --> 00:28:25,972
- [Joel] I need more calendars.

780
00:28:26,005 --> 00:28:27,407
- He wants to be alone.

781
00:28:27,440 --> 00:28:29,875
- Well this is the one
time he shouldn't be alone.

782
00:28:29,908 --> 00:28:31,543
- Doc, you've got me worried.

783
00:28:31,577 --> 00:28:32,745
- I didn't mean to scare you.

784
00:28:32,778 --> 00:28:35,448
This is a matter of the
greatest importance.

785
00:28:35,482 --> 00:28:36,648
- I'll try.

786
00:28:36,682 --> 00:28:38,852
- I'll be here until
I hear from you.

787
00:28:38,885 --> 00:28:41,053
Donna, everything all right?

788
00:28:41,087 --> 00:28:42,155
- [Tom] Well my
collar's too tight.

789
00:28:42,188 --> 00:28:43,557
- Yes Doc.

790
00:28:43,590 --> 00:28:45,392
- Bye.
- Goodbye.

791
00:28:48,260 --> 00:28:49,828
- [Joel] Boy, they're
good swimmers.

792
00:28:49,862 --> 00:28:50,730
(Marta screaming)

793
00:28:50,763 --> 00:28:51,597
- [Crow] Waders.

794
00:28:54,233 --> 00:28:55,702
See the sand is hot.

795
00:28:58,203 --> 00:29:00,373
- Boy, that water was cold.

796
00:29:00,407 --> 00:29:03,342
- [Tom] You know what
that means? (laughing)

797
00:29:04,277 --> 00:29:07,580
(Marta grunting)

798
00:29:07,613 --> 00:29:09,015
- All right, now.

799
00:29:10,183 --> 00:29:11,918
- [Joel] The biking,
then the marathon.

800
00:29:11,952 --> 00:29:14,820
- Now what?

801
00:29:14,853 --> 00:29:16,488
- The answer.

802
00:29:16,522 --> 00:29:17,357
- Answer?

803
00:29:18,223 --> 00:29:19,358
What answer?

804
00:29:19,392 --> 00:29:20,927
- [Joel] Take it easy.
- [Crow] Sorry.

805
00:29:20,960 --> 00:29:21,862
- Are you gonna stay here?

806
00:29:23,195 --> 00:29:24,597
- Well why should I do that?

807
00:29:26,532 --> 00:29:28,033
- So I can marry you, stupid.

808
00:29:28,067 --> 00:29:29,302
- Marry me?

809
00:29:29,335 --> 00:29:31,003
- [Tom] I'll marry you
stupid, smart, anyway.

810
00:29:32,538 --> 00:29:35,208
(Marta giggling)

811
00:29:35,242 --> 00:29:36,342
Whoa, he upended her.

812
00:29:37,277 --> 00:29:39,012
Hi, I'm Tom Servo.

813
00:29:39,045 --> 00:29:40,813
(Marta screaming)

814
00:29:40,847 --> 00:29:42,048
- [Crow] Hey, I found my watch.

815
00:29:42,082 --> 00:29:44,817
(dramatic music)

816
00:29:46,052 --> 00:29:49,422
- Paul, Paul, take
me away, please.

817
00:29:49,455 --> 00:29:50,757
- Sure baby, sure.

818
00:29:50,790 --> 00:29:53,560
(dramatic music)

819
00:29:55,962 --> 00:29:58,430
- [Joel] Did he just offer
his hand in marriage?

820
00:29:58,463 --> 00:30:00,200
- [Tom] Well, not
exactly his hand.

821
00:30:00,233 --> 00:30:01,800
- I'll lock it in
the trunk of the car.

822
00:30:01,835 --> 00:30:03,737
- No, no, I couldn't stand it.

823
00:30:03,770 --> 00:30:04,570
Let's go right to the sheriff.

824
00:30:04,603 --> 00:30:05,472
There's been a murder.

825
00:30:05,505 --> 00:30:06,805
- No, not a murder.

826
00:30:06,840 --> 00:30:08,742
- A human hand not a murder.

827
00:30:08,775 --> 00:30:10,743
- Listen, please.

828
00:30:10,777 --> 00:30:11,945
You've just got to trust me.

829
00:30:11,978 --> 00:30:13,312
If it's what I think it is,

830
00:30:13,345 --> 00:30:14,547
it could be very
important for me, please.

831
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:17,117
- No, no, just take
me away from here.

832
00:30:17,150 --> 00:30:20,187
- [Joel] I love her,
but I want the hand.

833
00:30:20,220 --> 00:30:21,922
I still love her though.

834
00:30:21,955 --> 00:30:23,523
And that hand would
be really great.

835
00:30:23,557 --> 00:30:26,158
But, I don't know what.

836
00:30:26,192 --> 00:30:28,260
- [Tom] Babe, hand, babe, hand.

837
00:30:29,628 --> 00:30:32,398
(dramatic music)

838
00:30:41,307 --> 00:30:42,975
- Put your hands
up, or I'll shoot

839
00:30:44,077 --> 00:30:45,345
- Oh, Mrs. Hotchkiss,
it's only me.

840
00:30:45,378 --> 00:30:46,745
- [Crow] It's Harriet
Nelson's evil twin.

841
00:30:46,778 --> 00:30:50,217
- You said you wouldn't
be home until late.

842
00:30:50,250 --> 00:30:52,085
- Oh, I just remembered
this old pile of papers.

843
00:30:52,118 --> 00:30:54,253
I thought I'd come back
and look something up.

844
00:30:54,287 --> 00:30:56,088
- Well, what'd you come
in the back door for?

845
00:30:56,122 --> 00:30:59,858
You know how jumpy I am
about noises in the house.

846
00:30:59,892 --> 00:31:02,128
I got Mr. Hotchkiss'
old gun out.

847
00:31:02,162 --> 00:31:05,365
I thought they'd carry a
burglar out of here tonight.

848
00:31:05,398 --> 00:31:07,633
- [Tom] Could I just take
one practice shot at you?

849
00:31:07,667 --> 00:31:08,835
I was kinda psyched.

850
00:31:08,868 --> 00:31:11,503
- I was gonna shoot,
and ask questions later.

851
00:31:11,537 --> 00:31:12,438
- [Tom] Just one shot.

852
00:31:12,472 --> 00:31:13,707
- It's got a hair trigger too.

853
00:31:15,275 --> 00:31:18,510
Well, as long as
it's you, it's okay.

854
00:31:18,543 --> 00:31:20,045
How 'bout some supper?

855
00:31:20,078 --> 00:31:21,413
- Oh, no thanks.

856
00:31:21,447 --> 00:31:24,183
- I have some stew left
over from last night.

857
00:31:24,217 --> 00:31:26,152
I could heat it up for ya.

858
00:31:26,185 --> 00:31:27,620
- No, I.

859
00:31:27,653 --> 00:31:29,422
- [Crow] If the gun doesn't
kill him, the stew will.

860
00:31:29,455 --> 00:31:30,923
- I'm not hungry really.

861
00:31:30,957 --> 00:31:32,825
I guess I'll just go up to
my room, if you don't mind.

862
00:31:32,858 --> 00:31:33,660
Good night.

863
00:31:33,693 --> 00:31:34,593
- Good night.

864
00:31:34,627 --> 00:31:35,395
- [Tom] Hey, come on, one shot.

865
00:31:35,428 --> 00:31:36,228
I'll just wing ya.

866
00:31:36,262 --> 00:31:37,530
It won't hurt that much.

867
00:31:42,568 --> 00:31:45,438
- [Joel] I've got
to have that hand.

868
00:31:45,472 --> 00:31:47,540
Can't eat, can't sleep.

869
00:31:47,573 --> 00:31:49,908
All I do is think about it.

870
00:31:49,942 --> 00:31:51,077
Maybe if there's something.

871
00:31:51,110 --> 00:31:52,878
Oh, no.

872
00:31:54,280 --> 00:31:55,848
- [Crow] Man, with an
extra hand around the house

873
00:31:55,882 --> 00:31:57,583
just think of the
stuff I could do.

874
00:32:01,887 --> 00:32:02,822
Just think.

875
00:32:06,325 --> 00:32:09,095
(waves crashing)

876
00:32:10,195 --> 00:32:13,298
- [Joel] Star
light, star bright,

877
00:32:13,332 --> 00:32:16,402
first hand I see tonight.

878
00:32:17,437 --> 00:32:19,205
I wish I may, I wish I might.

879
00:32:21,473 --> 00:32:22,375
(Paul whistling)

880
00:32:22,408 --> 00:32:24,177
- [Tom] Little human hand.

881
00:32:24,210 --> 00:32:25,077
Come here.

882
00:32:25,110 --> 00:32:26,412
Little human hand.

883
00:32:26,445 --> 00:32:27,280
Come on.

884
00:32:29,382 --> 00:32:33,418
Little human hand, yes.

885
00:32:33,452 --> 00:32:34,720
- [Crow] If you want
to catch a human hand,

886
00:32:34,753 --> 00:32:36,955
you got to think
like a human hand.

887
00:32:38,423 --> 00:32:41,160
(dramatic music)

888
00:32:43,763 --> 00:32:45,898
- [Joel] That's giant.

889
00:32:45,932 --> 00:32:47,867
That hand's unbelievably huge.

890
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:49,902
- [Crow] It's called
for shortening, Joel.

891
00:32:51,570 --> 00:32:53,740
- [Joel] Cootchie cootchie coo.

892
00:32:53,773 --> 00:32:55,575
Cootchie cootchie coo.

893
00:32:59,145 --> 00:33:02,215
- [Tom] You got
to hand it to him.

894
00:33:02,248 --> 00:33:03,717
- [Crow] Went out on
a limb with that one.

895
00:33:03,750 --> 00:33:04,883
- [Joel] It's a good thing

896
00:33:04,917 --> 00:33:06,952
he brought that
freezer wrap along.

897
00:33:06,985 --> 00:33:07,920
- [Tom] I don't think
it's freezer wrap.

898
00:33:07,953 --> 00:33:09,288
I think it's a handbag.

899
00:33:09,322 --> 00:33:11,890
(Tom laughing)

900
00:33:16,628 --> 00:33:17,763
- [Crow] Oh that'll go great

901
00:33:17,797 --> 00:33:18,865
with a little glass
of milk later.

902
00:33:18,898 --> 00:33:20,867
A little honey cured hand.

903
00:33:20,900 --> 00:33:22,802
(dramatic music)

904
00:33:22,835 --> 00:33:24,670
Next to the hand
cream on the shelf.

905
00:33:24,703 --> 00:33:27,640
- Yeah, Steve, it's positive.

906
00:33:27,673 --> 00:33:29,208
There is a reading.

907
00:33:29,242 --> 00:33:31,343
- [Crow] Yes, you've
proved that they are rats.

908
00:33:31,377 --> 00:33:32,845
- It was a positive reading.

909
00:33:32,878 --> 00:33:34,813
This may be all the evidence
we need to convince Berringer.

910
00:33:34,847 --> 00:33:35,748
- Oh, really?

911
00:33:35,782 --> 00:33:36,982
- [Tom] Oh these two guys.

912
00:33:37,015 --> 00:33:38,150
We're in for some
serious acting now.

913
00:33:38,183 --> 00:33:39,418
- Don't you understand?

914
00:33:39,452 --> 00:33:40,252
I'm giving you the answer.

915
00:33:40,285 --> 00:33:41,320
- It's too late, Doc.

916
00:33:42,222 --> 00:33:43,022
- What's that?

917
00:33:43,055 --> 00:33:44,423
- It's my resignation.

918
00:33:44,457 --> 00:33:45,357
- Oh, you talked to him?

919
00:33:45,425 --> 00:33:46,158
- Oh no, not quite.

920
00:33:46,225 --> 00:33:47,427
He talked.

921
00:33:47,460 --> 00:33:48,227
He said either get on
his wagon, or get out.

922
00:33:48,260 --> 00:33:49,162
Well I'm getting out.

923
00:33:49,228 --> 00:33:50,128
- Well now, wait a minute.

924
00:33:50,128 --> 00:33:51,530
Tear it up, you don't need it.

925
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:53,232
This is the gauge I've
been telling you about.

926
00:33:53,232 --> 00:33:55,635
It measures the
results of cosmic rays.

927
00:33:55,635 --> 00:33:57,737
Or the presence of
something associated with.

928
00:33:57,770 --> 00:33:59,438
- What is the something, Doc?

929
00:34:00,873 --> 00:34:02,040
- Well, I don't know,
- [Tom] It's a something.

930
00:34:02,075 --> 00:34:03,242
- But I did make a very
important discovery

931
00:34:03,242 --> 00:34:04,043
about Elmer here.

932
00:34:04,077 --> 00:34:05,343
- Has he got it?

933
00:34:05,377 --> 00:34:06,845
- Oh, very definitely.
- So how did he get it?

934
00:34:06,845 --> 00:34:07,847
- [Crow] Probably
at a night club.

935
00:34:07,847 --> 00:34:09,248
- Oh, I see.

936
00:34:09,282 --> 00:34:11,450
You're not aware of Elmer.

937
00:34:11,483 --> 00:34:13,052
He's a very distinguished rat.

938
00:34:13,085 --> 00:34:14,353
- [Joel] Perhaps you're
familiar with his glue.

939
00:34:14,353 --> 00:34:17,390
- He was a passenger on
our last space program,

940
00:34:17,457 --> 00:34:19,658
before we launched our
first manned rocket.

941
00:34:19,692 --> 00:34:22,628
In the beginning we thought
he was completely unaffected.

942
00:34:22,662 --> 00:34:23,562
- But he wasn't?

943
00:34:23,595 --> 00:34:25,097
- [Joel] I think the doctor's

944
00:34:25,130 --> 00:34:26,965
been spending a little too
much time in the rat lab.

945
00:34:26,998 --> 00:34:28,733
- I don't know when he'll
turn on the other rats

946
00:34:28,768 --> 00:34:30,235
and kill 'em.

947
00:34:30,268 --> 00:34:31,870
Now he can unlock
an ordinary cage.

948
00:34:31,903 --> 00:34:34,773
That's why I had that
padlock put on there.

949
00:34:34,807 --> 00:34:36,642
Everyone else around
here seems to think that

950
00:34:36,675 --> 00:34:39,412
what he does isn't in the range

951
00:34:39,445 --> 00:34:42,848
of an exceptional
rat's capabilities.

952
00:34:42,882 --> 00:34:43,783
But I don't.

953
00:34:43,817 --> 00:34:45,452
I think Elmer has thoughts.

954
00:34:45,485 --> 00:34:46,752
- [Tom] Naughty thoughts.

955
00:34:48,453 --> 00:34:49,355
- [Joel] Human thoughts.

956
00:34:49,388 --> 00:34:50,557
- Are they human?

957
00:34:50,590 --> 00:34:52,225
- [Tom] Better than
human thoughts.

958
00:34:53,758 --> 00:34:54,860
- Like this something.

959
00:34:55,962 --> 00:34:58,063
The same something
that was in our pilots.

960
00:34:58,097 --> 00:35:00,800
- Well Steve, there's
a possibility.

961
00:35:04,237 --> 00:35:06,572
(paper ripping)

962
00:35:06,605 --> 00:35:09,175
(upbeat music)

963
00:35:13,045 --> 00:35:18,017
(dramatic music)
(bottles clanking)

964
00:35:19,852 --> 00:35:21,320
- [Tom] It's W.C. Handy.

965
00:35:23,722 --> 00:35:25,357
(light switch clicking)

966
00:35:25,390 --> 00:35:27,427
- [Crow] Hey, she's
wearing a drool pad.

967
00:35:31,297 --> 00:35:34,267
- [Joel] She's putting on
Dr. Spock's house coat.

968
00:35:37,002 --> 00:35:38,003
- [Tom] Doctor or mister?

969
00:35:38,037 --> 00:35:39,572
- [Joel] Mister.

970
00:35:39,605 --> 00:35:41,207
- [Crow] Dr. Mister?

971
00:35:41,240 --> 00:35:43,742
- [Tom] Mr. Mister.

972
00:35:43,775 --> 00:35:45,043
- [Joel] Mr. Misdemeanor.

973
00:35:46,378 --> 00:35:47,513
- [Tom] That's what you get

974
00:35:47,547 --> 00:35:49,015
when you rob a
Dairy Queen, right?

975
00:35:49,048 --> 00:35:51,383
- [Crow] Wow, she's pretty
quick with that cold steel.

976
00:35:51,417 --> 00:35:53,352
- [Joel] Nuts in her girdle.

977
00:35:53,385 --> 00:35:55,253
- [Tom] Good thing
she has a handgun.

978
00:36:00,392 --> 00:36:01,193
Cujo?

979
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:03,528
- [Joel] Hey boy.

980
00:36:03,562 --> 00:36:05,430
- [Crow] Hey, come
on, this isn't funny.

981
00:36:05,463 --> 00:36:06,998
- [Tom] Guys?

982
00:36:07,032 --> 00:36:07,867
Guys?

983
00:36:12,137 --> 00:36:12,938
- [Crow] Don't take that off.

984
00:36:12,972 --> 00:36:13,805
Your chin'll explode.

985
00:36:17,977 --> 00:36:19,478
- Jackson.

986
00:36:19,512 --> 00:36:21,280
Have you been making
this noise out here?

987
00:36:21,313 --> 00:36:22,215
Come on, get down.

988
00:36:22,248 --> 00:36:23,015
You know better than that.

989
00:36:23,048 --> 00:36:25,517
(cat meowing)

990
00:36:27,687 --> 00:36:30,355
- [Crow] Oops, sorry
Jackson, was that your head?

991
00:36:30,388 --> 00:36:32,658
- What is this doing out here?

992
00:36:32,692 --> 00:36:33,725
(cat meowing)

993
00:36:33,758 --> 00:36:35,260
- [Tom] Just kinda lying there.

994
00:36:36,828 --> 00:36:38,797
(light switch clicking)

995
00:36:38,830 --> 00:36:39,765
(cat meowing)

996
00:36:39,798 --> 00:36:41,400
- Oh, what a mess.

997
00:36:42,935 --> 00:36:44,170
- [Joel] A chocolate mess.

998
00:36:44,203 --> 00:36:45,805
- That boy.

999
00:36:45,838 --> 00:36:48,540
(gun clattering)

1000
00:36:48,573 --> 00:36:51,077
(cat meowing)

1001
00:36:55,815 --> 00:36:58,617
He said he wasn't hungry,
and then he comes downstairs

1002
00:36:58,650 --> 00:37:01,020
and knocks these jars
off of the shelf.

1003
00:37:01,053 --> 00:37:02,722
- [Tom] That proves he's hungry.

1004
00:37:02,755 --> 00:37:04,257
- My jelly out to do that.

1005
00:37:06,325 --> 00:37:08,293
[Joel] Oh, Helga, that tickles.

1006
00:37:09,428 --> 00:37:12,197
(dramatic music)

1007
00:37:14,633 --> 00:37:16,635
- [Crow] Hey, it's
the title character.

1008
00:37:18,770 --> 00:37:20,338
- Cleaning after this kid, god.

1009
00:37:24,643 --> 00:37:25,543
- [Crow] I recognize him.

1010
00:37:25,577 --> 00:37:27,145
He used to be with Def Leppard.

1011
00:37:27,178 --> 00:37:29,482
- [Tom] Jackson, get
away from that ectoplasm.

1012
00:37:32,818 --> 00:37:33,718
- That boy.

1013
00:37:34,853 --> 00:37:36,455
Wait'll I get ahold
of him tomorrow.

1014
00:37:37,523 --> 00:37:39,525
Why the shower curtain?

1015
00:37:39,558 --> 00:37:41,227
- [Crow] That's a good
question for anyone.

1016
00:37:50,235 --> 00:37:52,438
- [Tom] (grunting)
Someone better call Madge,

1017
00:37:52,472 --> 00:37:54,040
the Palmolive case worker.

1018
00:37:59,278 --> 00:38:02,013
- Trouble waking me up in
the middle of the night.

1019
00:38:02,047 --> 00:38:05,752
As if I haven't
had enough of it.

1020
00:38:05,785 --> 00:38:07,118
(bottle slamming)

1021
00:38:07,152 --> 00:38:08,253
Empty.

1022
00:38:08,287 --> 00:38:10,022
- [Tom] Good night, sweet troll.

1023
00:38:10,055 --> 00:38:13,058
- [Crow] She needs about
150 hours of beauty sleep.

1024
00:38:23,835 --> 00:38:27,238
(Joel, Tom, and Crow crying)

1025
00:38:27,272 --> 00:38:28,640
- [Crow] Oh no, look out.

1026
00:38:28,673 --> 00:38:32,578
(Joel, Tom, and Crow sighing)

1027
00:38:32,612 --> 00:38:34,547
- [Tom] They got us on that one.

1028
00:38:34,580 --> 00:38:36,082
- [Crow] I knew it was coming.

1029
00:38:39,918 --> 00:38:41,120
- [Joel] I can't believe

1030
00:38:41,153 --> 00:38:43,255
she's got to do that
scene over again.

1031
00:38:43,288 --> 00:38:46,058
(dramatic music)

1032
00:38:49,695 --> 00:38:51,597
- [Tom] Back into the kitchen.

1033
00:38:51,630 --> 00:38:53,998
- [Crow] She forgot to
put her chin hammock on.

1034
00:38:55,735 --> 00:38:57,770
Hey, what's that stuff?

1035
00:38:57,803 --> 00:38:59,172
- [Tom] It's the nighttime,
coughing, aching,

1036
00:38:59,205 --> 00:39:00,707
sneezing, stuffy head, fever,

1037
00:39:00,740 --> 00:39:02,808
so you're being chased
by a human hand medicine.

1038
00:39:12,183 --> 00:39:13,585
- [Joel] Good.

1039
00:39:13,618 --> 00:39:16,355
(dramatic music)

1040
00:39:17,890 --> 00:39:19,092
- [Tom] You know things are bad

1041
00:39:19,125 --> 00:39:21,127
when you keep a liquor
cabinet in your bedroom.

1042
00:39:21,160 --> 00:39:22,495
- That oughtta do it.

1043
00:39:23,862 --> 00:39:24,963
- [Tom] For a horse.

1044
00:39:33,905 --> 00:39:35,140
- [Crow] Oh Helga.

1045
00:39:35,173 --> 00:39:36,375
Oh stewardesses.

1046
00:39:36,408 --> 00:39:39,312
Me, you, chandelier, swinging.

1047
00:39:48,287 --> 00:39:49,988
- [Joel] I can't believe
she's gonna do this scene

1048
00:39:50,022 --> 00:39:51,223
one more time.

1049
00:39:51,257 --> 00:39:53,125
They used to call
her One Take Sally,

1050
00:39:53,158 --> 00:39:55,393
and now, look, nothing.

1051
00:39:55,427 --> 00:39:56,962
- [Tom] Let's see, if I
look in the dictionary,

1052
00:39:56,995 --> 00:39:59,132
I think that's where
I keep my stash.

1053
00:40:07,005 --> 00:40:08,473
- [Joel] Oh, I've seen this.

1054
00:40:08,507 --> 00:40:10,575
This is when she does her
Judy Garland impression.

1055
00:40:14,213 --> 00:40:15,915
(Mrs. Hotchkiss gagging)

1056
00:40:15,948 --> 00:40:17,817
- [Tom] What the?

1057
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:20,085
- [Crow] No, I think she's
doing Mrs. Captain Kirk.

1058
00:40:20,118 --> 00:40:21,787
(Mrs. Hotchkiss gagging)

1059
00:40:21,820 --> 00:40:24,790
- [Tom] Got to find push button.

1060
00:40:26,058 --> 00:40:27,258
Must lay down.

1061
00:40:30,797 --> 00:40:32,665
Must perform death scene.

1062
00:40:35,100 --> 00:40:36,068
- [Crow] Oh, grab your pills.

1063
00:40:36,102 --> 00:40:37,068
That'll help.

1064
00:40:37,102 --> 00:40:39,838
(dramatic music)

1065
00:40:39,872 --> 00:40:41,840
(Mrs. Hotchkiss coughing)
(gun blasting)

1066
00:40:41,873 --> 00:40:42,675
- [Joel] Helga?

1067
00:40:47,345 --> 00:40:48,747
- Mrs. Hotchkiss?

1068
00:40:56,222 --> 00:40:57,422
Mrs. Hotchkiss?

1069
00:41:01,427 --> 00:41:04,128
- [Tom] She's a victim
of a Hotchkiss disease.

1070
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:09,868
- [Crow] I bet he's
gonna be all choked up,

1071
00:41:09,902 --> 00:41:11,603
now that she's dead.

1072
00:41:11,637 --> 00:41:12,738
All choked up, get it?

1073
00:41:17,042 --> 00:41:20,712
- [Tom] And now, a really
big hand for the little lady.

1074
00:41:20,745 --> 00:41:23,882
Mrs. Hotchkiss, right
here on our very floor.

1075
00:41:23,915 --> 00:41:26,718
- [Joel] Rings, fingernails,
clippers, lubriderm lotion?

1076
00:41:26,752 --> 00:41:27,587
It can't be.

1077
00:41:28,520 --> 00:41:29,355
No.

1078
00:41:34,860 --> 00:41:35,693
It's a clue.

1079
00:41:40,900 --> 00:41:41,700
- [Tom] Hey, little hand?

1080
00:41:41,733 --> 00:41:43,368
Little hand?

1081
00:41:43,402 --> 00:41:46,105
(dramatic music)

1082
00:41:51,143 --> 00:41:52,410
- [Crow] A big old booger.

1083
00:41:55,713 --> 00:41:57,950
- [Joel] This goes in
the jar with the others.

1084
00:42:03,988 --> 00:42:06,458
- Operator, get me
the sheriff, quick.

1085
00:42:07,927 --> 00:42:08,560
- [Tom] If he's not there,
I'll talk to The Skipper.

1086
00:42:08,593 --> 00:42:11,163
(phone ringing)

1087
00:42:12,397 --> 00:42:16,102
♪ Shaving, shaving,
la, la, la, la, la ♪

1088
00:42:16,135 --> 00:42:18,637
- Who?

1089
00:42:18,670 --> 00:42:19,505
Paul?

1090
00:42:21,040 --> 00:42:22,207
Paul Lawrence?

1091
00:42:23,075 --> 00:42:24,577
Will you stop talking so fast.

1092
00:42:24,610 --> 00:42:27,178
I can't understand a
word you're saying.

1093
00:42:27,212 --> 00:42:28,013
Stop shouting.

1094
00:42:28,047 --> 00:42:30,682
(Tom stammering)

1095
00:42:32,117 --> 00:42:33,218
Hotchkiss?

1096
00:42:33,252 --> 00:42:35,120
- [Tom] Yes, Mrs. Hotchkiss.

1097
00:42:35,153 --> 00:42:36,522
- Now don't touch anything.

1098
00:42:36,555 --> 00:42:37,590
- [Tom] I won't, just come.

1099
00:42:37,623 --> 00:42:38,957
- I'll be there in five minutes.

1100
00:42:38,990 --> 00:42:39,992
- [Tom] Oh hurry, please.

1101
00:42:40,025 --> 00:42:42,427
- Paul, I know how you feel son.

1102
00:42:42,460 --> 00:42:43,662
- [Tom] No you don't.

1103
00:42:43,695 --> 00:42:45,130
- I'll get there
as fast as I can.

1104
00:42:47,065 --> 00:42:48,233
- [Crow] He doesn't
know how he feels.

1105
00:42:48,267 --> 00:42:49,402
He's never been
attacked by a hand.

1106
00:42:49,435 --> 00:42:51,103
He has no idea how he feels.

1107
00:42:52,003 --> 00:42:53,205
Look at that boy.

1108
00:42:53,238 --> 00:42:55,173
He's got a ball of pain inside.

1109
00:42:57,175 --> 00:42:59,412
- Try and raise the
county coroner, Earl.

1110
00:42:59,445 --> 00:43:01,147
And call my wife.

1111
00:43:01,180 --> 00:43:02,313
Tell her I'm liable
to be up all night.

1112
00:43:02,347 --> 00:43:03,715
- [Joel] Oh, we'd
better get out of here.

1113
00:43:03,748 --> 00:43:04,983
- [Tom] I'll call his wife.

1114
00:43:05,017 --> 00:43:06,252
- Sure thing Ted.

1115
00:43:06,285 --> 00:43:07,185
- [Tom] I'll tell
her she's married

1116
00:43:07,218 --> 00:43:08,553
to a two bit character actor.

1117
00:43:11,823 --> 00:43:14,427
(upbeat music)

1118
00:43:18,463 --> 00:43:20,798
(funky music)
- Got to think.

1119
00:43:20,832 --> 00:43:24,403
Must get back to Galileo 7.

1120
00:43:24,437 --> 00:43:27,907
Lives of 400 crew
members hang in balance.

1121
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:29,675
(Joel grunting)

1122
00:43:29,708 --> 00:43:31,443
- Vision fading.

1123
00:43:31,477 --> 00:43:34,447
Must cut transponder from wrist.

1124
00:43:34,480 --> 00:43:38,950
Fashion crude phaser device
with bed slat and light bulb.

1125
00:43:38,983 --> 00:43:40,818
- Must control myself.

1126
00:43:40,852 --> 00:43:44,088
Must find pastel
colored native female.

1127
00:43:44,122 --> 00:43:46,225
Execute Prime
Directive. (screaming)

1128
00:43:46,258 --> 00:43:49,828
- (grunting) Hand's
so young looking.

1129
00:43:49,862 --> 00:43:53,932
Mrs. Burg I thought you
were dead. (screaming)

1130
00:43:53,965 --> 00:43:55,867
- Throat scratchy.

1131
00:43:55,900 --> 00:43:59,572
Hand's so dry can scratch
name into it. (grunting)

1132
00:43:59,605 --> 00:44:03,208
- Embarrassing cuticles
on finger nails.

1133
00:44:03,242 --> 00:44:06,645
Must utilize emotional
memory and get to.

1134
00:44:06,678 --> 00:44:08,247
- Listen, (mumbling),
stop it, stop it.

1135
00:44:08,280 --> 00:44:09,382
- Stop?

1136
00:44:09,415 --> 00:44:10,615
That was my big scene, Joel.

1137
00:44:10,648 --> 00:44:12,150
- Hey, yeah, we were
just getting into it.

1138
00:44:12,183 --> 00:44:13,418
- This just isn't working.

1139
00:44:13,452 --> 00:44:15,587
I don't know how Shatner
does it week after week.

1140
00:44:15,620 --> 00:44:18,157
- I don't know why he does
it or who lets him do it.

1141
00:44:18,190 --> 00:44:20,192
- I think we should
just be glad he does it.

1142
00:44:20,225 --> 00:44:21,027
(alarm buzzing)

1143
00:44:21,060 --> 00:44:22,193
- Oh, we got movie sign!

1144
00:44:22,227 --> 00:44:23,795
Let's go!

1145
00:44:23,828 --> 00:44:25,163
- [Tom] Must get to theater.

1146
00:44:25,197 --> 00:44:28,133
(mysterious music)

1147
00:44:39,678 --> 00:44:40,778
- [Joel] That hand killed me.

1148
00:44:40,812 --> 00:44:42,013
It was hilarious.

1149
00:44:44,215 --> 00:44:45,283
- [Crow] Pretty funny.

1150
00:44:47,185 --> 00:44:49,722
- [Tom] We have to play
more games like that.

1151
00:44:49,755 --> 00:44:51,223
I like role playing games.

1152
00:44:59,998 --> 00:45:02,533
- You know, this book
of lists has everything.

1153
00:45:02,567 --> 00:45:04,035
Look at here.

1154
00:45:04,068 --> 00:45:05,837
What to do when a rogue
hand kills your land lady.

1155
00:45:07,372 --> 00:45:08,807
Roddy McDowell, Milton Berle?

1156
00:45:33,298 --> 00:45:35,667
- Operator, I'd like to make
a long distance call, please.

1157
00:45:35,700 --> 00:45:36,602
- [Tom] Well, I'll
do what I can.

1158
00:45:36,635 --> 00:45:37,803
But I can't promise anything.

1159
00:45:37,837 --> 00:45:40,338
You know how things
are. (mumbling)

1160
00:45:40,372 --> 00:45:42,307
- Never mind, operator.

1161
00:45:46,010 --> 00:45:46,812
Sheriff.

1162
00:45:46,845 --> 00:45:48,247
- [Joel] Skipper.

1163
00:45:48,280 --> 00:45:50,182
Where's the little
body, little buddy?

1164
00:45:55,387 --> 00:45:57,155
- I heard a shot.

1165
00:45:57,188 --> 00:45:58,190
I ran down from my room.

1166
00:45:58,223 --> 00:45:59,323
I found her like this.

1167
00:46:01,360 --> 00:46:03,095
- No signs of a bullet.

1168
00:46:03,128 --> 00:46:04,362
- Did you hear anything else,

1169
00:46:04,395 --> 00:46:05,630
like a door closing
or somebody running?

1170
00:46:05,663 --> 00:46:07,098
- [Crow] Or the sound
of one hand clapping?

1171
00:46:07,132 --> 00:46:07,967
- No, nothing.

1172
00:46:10,635 --> 00:46:11,837
- Looks like she's
been strangled.

1173
00:46:11,870 --> 00:46:13,405
You can see the
marks on her throat.

1174
00:46:13,438 --> 00:46:14,405
No doubt about it.

1175
00:46:16,842 --> 00:46:18,677
- [Joel] This lamp
has panties on it.

1176
00:46:19,712 --> 00:46:20,945
- Now tell me boy.

1177
00:46:20,978 --> 00:46:23,215
Did you ever see
this gun before?

1178
00:46:23,248 --> 00:46:25,583
- It belongs to Mr. Hotchkiss.

1179
00:46:25,617 --> 00:46:28,753
She told me that it
had a hair trigger.

1180
00:46:29,988 --> 00:46:31,423
- [Tom] Smells like
a hair trigger.

1181
00:46:31,457 --> 00:46:33,225
- Yes, it probably fired
when it fell off the table.

1182
00:46:33,258 --> 00:46:35,227
Now I know this is
hard on you, boy,

1183
00:46:35,260 --> 00:46:37,628
but anything you can
remember will help.

1184
00:46:37,662 --> 00:46:39,430
Anything at all.

1185
00:46:39,463 --> 00:46:40,798
Now do you recall,

1186
00:46:40,832 --> 00:46:42,533
did she leave any
valuables around the house?

1187
00:46:42,567 --> 00:46:44,268
Any sizeable amounts of money?

1188
00:46:45,670 --> 00:46:47,538
- [Crow] Nothing that I found
rifling through her stuff.

1189
00:46:47,572 --> 00:46:49,542
- Not that I know.

1190
00:46:49,575 --> 00:46:51,443
- Well that isn't
the rumor has it.

1191
00:46:51,477 --> 00:46:53,145
That is, if you can
believe my wife, Edith.

1192
00:46:53,178 --> 00:46:55,513
And you can't always do that.

1193
00:46:55,547 --> 00:46:57,515
- [Joel] So then rumor
wouldn't have it.

1194
00:46:58,850 --> 00:47:00,885
- That'll probably be Earl.

1195
00:47:00,918 --> 00:47:02,353
- Oh, I'll get the door.

1196
00:47:02,387 --> 00:47:03,222
- [Tom] Wow.

1197
00:47:04,823 --> 00:47:05,823
Psychic.

1198
00:47:08,593 --> 00:47:12,430
(Joel, Tom, and Crow meowing)

1199
00:47:17,602 --> 00:47:18,837
- That should do the trick.

1200
00:47:18,870 --> 00:47:20,438
At least that's what
my wife Edith says,

1201
00:47:20,472 --> 00:47:22,007
it usually does for her.

1202
00:47:22,040 --> 00:47:23,475
Two of those and a
glass of warm milk

1203
00:47:23,508 --> 00:47:24,710
and she's out like a light.

1204
00:47:24,743 --> 00:47:26,312
- [Joel] Make sure you
drink it like a raccoon.

1205
00:47:26,312 --> 00:47:28,947
- We'll go over the routine
questions in the morning.

1206
00:47:29,013 --> 00:47:30,648
Now Paul, just one other thing.

1207
00:47:31,850 --> 00:47:33,452
Maybe you don't want to
stay here in the house.

1208
00:47:33,518 --> 00:47:34,720
I could drive you
over to my house,

1209
00:47:34,753 --> 00:47:36,355
and we'll find you
another place tomorrow.

1210
00:47:36,422 --> 00:47:37,555
- [Crow] I've got a
couple of hammocks,

1211
00:47:37,622 --> 00:47:38,823
and a sailor's hat
I'd like you to wear.

1212
00:47:38,857 --> 00:47:40,125
- No, I'll be all right.

1213
00:47:40,158 --> 00:47:41,860
Don't worry about it.

1214
00:47:41,927 --> 00:47:43,828
- Soap and water will
take that ink off.

1215
00:47:43,862 --> 00:47:46,232
You better go upstairs,
and try and get some sleep.

1216
00:47:50,935 --> 00:47:52,870
- [Joel] Strange kid, dipping
his hands in ink like that.

1217
00:47:52,938 --> 00:47:53,838
How do you figure.

1218
00:47:53,838 --> 00:47:55,540
- All through downstairs then.

1219
00:47:55,573 --> 00:47:56,742
- Think you got it (mumbling)?

1220
00:47:56,775 --> 00:47:58,643
- Well, most of the
prints were pretty small

1221
00:47:58,677 --> 00:48:00,078
They seemed to match the
ones we got off the gun

1222
00:48:00,145 --> 00:48:01,247
and the flashlight.

1223
00:48:01,280 --> 00:48:02,948
- [Crow] Prince is pretty small.

1224
00:48:02,948 --> 00:48:04,950
- Yeah, I think so,
but I can't tell yet.

1225
00:48:06,150 --> 00:48:08,487
Oh, there was one that
might be interesting.

1226
00:48:08,520 --> 00:48:10,888
Got it off a medallion
she was wearing.

1227
00:48:10,922 --> 00:48:12,357
- How soon can we get a report?

1228
00:48:12,390 --> 00:48:14,525
- Well, I'll telephoto a
set of these to Washington,

1229
00:48:14,558 --> 00:48:16,027
and run a make on 'em here.

1230
00:48:16,060 --> 00:48:17,963
We ought to know something
in, oh, five, six hours.

1231
00:48:20,598 --> 00:48:21,633
What about the kid?

1232
00:48:23,102 --> 00:48:24,302
- Earl I don't know.

1233
00:48:24,335 --> 00:48:26,638
I don't like to guess
on a case like this.

1234
00:48:26,672 --> 00:48:28,940
I just don't understand why
he wants to stay in the house.

1235
00:48:28,973 --> 00:48:30,675
I should think he'd want to
get as far away from here

1236
00:48:30,708 --> 00:48:31,610
as possible.

1237
00:48:31,643 --> 00:48:32,843
- Ah, he's a medical student.

1238
00:48:32,877 --> 00:48:33,945
He's used to being
around dead bodies.

1239
00:48:33,978 --> 00:48:35,347
- I suppose.

1240
00:48:35,380 --> 00:48:36,147
- [Tom] Just like us.

1241
00:48:36,180 --> 00:48:37,315
- Well come on.

1242
00:48:37,348 --> 00:48:39,050
We've got a full night's
work ahead of us.

1243
00:48:39,083 --> 00:48:41,520
- Yeah, maybe if we're lucky
we'll get an hour of sleep.

1244
00:48:41,553 --> 00:48:42,453
(Sheriff laughing)

1245
00:48:42,487 --> 00:48:43,222
- [Tom] Yeah, nice improv.

1246
00:48:44,388 --> 00:48:47,125
Coconut cream pie, it
just doesn't figure.

1247
00:48:47,158 --> 00:48:48,460
That cat is thirsty.

1248
00:48:51,228 --> 00:48:52,530
- [Crow] His pants shrunk.

1249
00:48:53,698 --> 00:48:55,967
- [Joel] Now to catch a hand,

1250
00:48:56,000 --> 00:48:59,237
you've got to think like a hand.

1251
00:48:59,270 --> 00:49:01,373
I'll lay out some
press on nails.

1252
00:49:02,240 --> 00:49:03,308
Yeah, that would work.

1253
00:49:03,342 --> 00:49:04,843
Or maybe some lotion.

1254
00:49:04,877 --> 00:49:06,077
Lubriderm or?

1255
00:49:07,245 --> 00:49:08,980
Yeah, I know, some
Isotoner gloves.

1256
00:49:09,013 --> 00:49:10,382
That would work great.

1257
00:49:10,415 --> 00:49:11,683
Oh magic lamp.

1258
00:49:11,717 --> 00:49:13,318
Help me find the hand.

1259
00:49:14,753 --> 00:49:15,787
- Doctor Weitzberg.

1260
00:49:15,820 --> 00:49:16,655
Weitzberg.

1261
00:49:22,760 --> 00:49:24,195
- [Joel] Hello, operator, hello.

1262
00:49:24,228 --> 00:49:25,930
- Operator, long
distance, please.

1263
00:49:25,963 --> 00:49:26,932
- [Joel] Long distance to where?

1264
00:49:26,965 --> 00:49:29,668
Where would you like to call to?

1265
00:49:29,702 --> 00:49:34,205
- I'd like to call
Doctor Max Weitzberg.

1266
00:49:34,238 --> 00:49:35,573
- [Joel] Weitzberg, how
would you spell that?

1267
00:49:35,607 --> 00:49:37,042
Washington, D.C.

1268
00:49:37,075 --> 00:49:38,677
- No, no, operator, I
don't have the number.

1269
00:49:38,710 --> 00:49:39,912
- [Joel] Can you spell the name?

1270
00:49:39,945 --> 00:49:40,912
- Listen, operator,
this is an emergency.

1271
00:49:40,945 --> 00:49:42,180
- [Joel] It's an emergency?

1272
00:49:42,213 --> 00:49:43,248
- No, I'll hold on, okay.

1273
00:49:43,282 --> 00:49:44,582
- [Joel] Could you hold on?

1274
00:49:45,517 --> 00:49:47,585
How do you spell that name?

1275
00:49:47,618 --> 00:49:49,487
- No, Weitzberg.

1276
00:49:49,520 --> 00:49:51,055
- [Joel] Weitzberg? (mumbling)

1277
00:49:51,088 --> 00:49:51,890
- W.
- [Joel] W.

1278
00:49:51,923 --> 00:49:52,790
- E.
- [Joel] W-E.

1279
00:49:53,825 --> 00:49:55,127
- I-T-Z.

1280
00:49:55,160 --> 00:49:56,327
- [Joel] I-T-Z, gotcha.

1281
00:49:56,362 --> 00:49:58,063
- B.
- [Joel] Keep trucking.

1282
00:49:58,097 --> 00:49:59,363
- E.
- [Joel] I can hear you,

1283
00:49:59,397 --> 00:50:01,533
but you're fading.

1284
00:50:01,567 --> 00:50:03,000
Are you on drugs?

1285
00:50:03,035 --> 00:50:05,503
Would you like to
call the drug hotline?

1286
00:50:05,537 --> 00:50:06,972
Are you tripping?

1287
00:50:08,540 --> 00:50:10,175
Be on the lookout
for a crawling hand.

1288
00:50:10,208 --> 00:50:13,878
Crawls up on the banister,
and then drops on people.

1289
00:50:13,912 --> 00:50:16,415
Are you sure you can
hold on the line?

1290
00:50:16,448 --> 00:50:19,150
(dramatic music)

1291
00:50:21,185 --> 00:50:23,188
Hello, I can't hear you.

1292
00:50:26,123 --> 00:50:27,358
It tends to drop on people

1293
00:50:27,392 --> 00:50:30,128
right when they are
about to pass out.

1294
00:50:30,162 --> 00:50:32,730
(Tom groaning)

1295
00:50:35,767 --> 00:50:36,568
- [Crow] Wow!

1296
00:50:36,602 --> 00:50:38,370
(dramatic music)

1297
00:50:38,403 --> 00:50:40,072
- [Tom] This is action packed.

1298
00:50:40,105 --> 00:50:41,038
(Paul screaming)

1299
00:50:41,072 --> 00:50:41,907
- [Crow] Come on, spit it out.

1300
00:50:41,940 --> 00:50:43,040
Cough it up.

1301
00:50:45,443 --> 00:50:48,080
(siren blaring)

1302
00:50:53,352 --> 00:50:54,385
First word.

1303
00:50:54,418 --> 00:50:56,253
Sounds like smoke.

1304
00:50:56,287 --> 00:50:58,923
(Paul grunting)

1305
00:51:08,300 --> 00:51:09,733
- [Joel] Cootchie
cootchie cootchie.

1306
00:51:09,768 --> 00:51:10,568
Cootchie cootchie coo.

1307
00:51:10,602 --> 00:51:12,070
Playdough face.

1308
00:51:12,103 --> 00:51:14,538
Playdough face.

1309
00:51:14,572 --> 00:51:17,242
(Joel mumbling)

1310
00:51:17,275 --> 00:51:18,710
Did he just give
him a wet willie?

1311
00:51:18,743 --> 00:51:19,878
- [Tom] I think he did.

1312
00:51:21,547 --> 00:51:24,715
With his own saliva,
pretty tricky.

1313
00:51:24,748 --> 00:51:26,183
- Boy, I sure could use a beer.

1314
00:51:26,217 --> 00:51:28,220
- Who couldn't, but
we can't stop now.

1315
00:51:28,253 --> 00:51:29,487
- Bet there's some
in the kitchen.

1316
00:51:29,520 --> 00:51:30,888
- Not me, not here.

1317
00:51:30,922 --> 00:51:31,723
- [Tom] Me neither.

1318
00:51:31,757 --> 00:51:33,725
- A nice cool beer.

1319
00:51:33,758 --> 00:51:35,393
- [Tom] A tall, frosty one.

1320
00:51:35,427 --> 00:51:36,962
- Hundreds of miles away by now.

1321
00:51:36,995 --> 00:51:39,363
Just think of that
long, tall, foamy brew.

1322
00:51:39,397 --> 00:51:40,565
- [Joel] Yowser.

1323
00:51:40,598 --> 00:51:41,800
- You could talk a
snake out of his skin.

1324
00:51:41,833 --> 00:51:43,202
Okay, let's take a look.

1325
00:51:43,235 --> 00:51:44,937
With my luck she
won't even have any.

1326
00:51:44,970 --> 00:51:47,305
- Dames like her always
keep beer around.

1327
00:51:47,338 --> 00:51:48,840
- [Joel] Always.

1328
00:51:48,873 --> 00:51:50,242
- I told you there'll be
beer in the refrigerator.

1329
00:51:52,410 --> 00:51:53,278
Holy!

1330
00:51:53,312 --> 00:51:54,245
Come here!

1331
00:51:54,278 --> 00:51:55,513
- What's the matter?

1332
00:51:55,547 --> 00:51:56,982
- [Tom] It's a two
for one special.

1333
00:51:57,015 --> 00:51:58,317
- I don't know, but I'm
getting out of here.

1334
00:51:58,350 --> 00:51:59,550
- [Crow] It's a body, dummy.

1335
00:51:59,583 --> 00:52:00,852
- Wait a minute, he
might still be alive.

1336
00:52:00,885 --> 00:52:02,520
- [Tom] No, it's not
a dummy, it's a body.

1337
00:52:02,553 --> 00:52:04,055
- Listen, buddy, we were
supposed to collect the dame,

1338
00:52:04,088 --> 00:52:04,890
and that's all.

1339
00:52:04,923 --> 00:52:05,990
This guy's on his own.

1340
00:52:06,023 --> 00:52:06,925
- He is alive.

1341
00:52:06,958 --> 00:52:08,427
Let's get him out of here.

1342
00:52:08,460 --> 00:52:09,360
- [Tom] You know, it doesn't
get any better than this.

1343
00:52:09,393 --> 00:52:11,530
(siren blaring)

1344
00:52:11,563 --> 00:52:13,265
- [Joel] You spend a
hard day loading bodies,

1345
00:52:13,298 --> 00:52:15,567
and you got one more
than you bargained for.

1346
00:52:15,600 --> 00:52:16,968
It's Miller time.

1347
00:52:21,673 --> 00:52:23,708
- [Tom] (mumbling) I was
making a long distance call,

1348
00:52:23,742 --> 00:52:26,077
and then, all I remember is.

1349
00:52:28,680 --> 00:52:30,482
(Paul screaming)
I slept with that!

1350
00:52:30,515 --> 00:52:33,185
- [Crow] Wow, he really musta
had his beer goggles on.

1351
00:52:33,218 --> 00:52:35,120
(Paul screaming)

1352
00:52:35,153 --> 00:52:38,088
(tires screeching)

1353
00:52:40,558 --> 00:52:41,827
- [Joel] Oh, he's high.

1354
00:52:45,330 --> 00:52:46,798
- [Tom] There goes the deposit.

1355
00:52:46,832 --> 00:52:49,333
- [Crow] This happens every
time you work with a live one.

1356
00:52:49,367 --> 00:52:50,468
- Of course I don't believe it.

1357
00:52:50,502 --> 00:52:52,003
Who could?

1358
00:52:52,037 --> 00:52:53,938
And don't think I'm gonna
let some fantastic story

1359
00:52:53,972 --> 00:52:56,173
about a ghost, fingerprints
or no fingerprints,

1360
00:52:56,207 --> 00:52:57,608
stop me from carrying
out this project.

1361
00:52:57,642 --> 00:52:58,577
- Oh now wait a minute.

1362
00:52:58,610 --> 00:52:59,610
- I'm not waiting for anything.

1363
00:52:59,643 --> 00:53:01,278
- [Tom] Calm down, Bucky.

1364
00:53:01,312 --> 00:53:03,282
- I was given a goal
and a time limit,

1365
00:53:03,315 --> 00:53:04,983
and that time limit
has just about run out.

1366
00:53:05,017 --> 00:53:06,785
- Lee, I think we're forgetting
the facts of the situation.

1367
00:53:06,818 --> 00:53:08,052
I know it all may
sound rather trivial,

1368
00:53:08,085 --> 00:53:09,287
with all of your problems here,

1369
00:53:09,320 --> 00:53:10,522
but the fact of
the matter remains

1370
00:53:10,555 --> 00:53:12,223
that a murder has
been committed.

1371
00:53:12,257 --> 00:53:13,825
And the prime
suspect in this case

1372
00:53:13,858 --> 00:53:15,627
happens to be one of our men.

1373
00:53:15,660 --> 00:53:17,328
- [Tom] This was no
suicide, it was murder.

1374
00:53:17,362 --> 00:53:20,798
- I gather from the attitude
of Mr, Mydell of security,

1375
00:53:20,832 --> 00:53:23,368
that it won't be long before
he applies real pressure

1376
00:53:23,402 --> 00:53:24,602
right here.

1377
00:53:24,635 --> 00:53:25,637
We may find ourselves
right in the middle

1378
00:53:25,670 --> 00:53:26,872
of an investigation.

1379
00:53:26,905 --> 00:53:28,873
- However, you do have
an alternative, Lee.

1380
00:53:28,907 --> 00:53:31,142
When Mydell calls,
you can gain time

1381
00:53:31,175 --> 00:53:33,578
by simply stating that
Lockhart had no authorization

1382
00:53:33,612 --> 00:53:35,513
to be in California,
and at the present time

1383
00:53:35,547 --> 00:53:37,548
you haven't the slightest
idea where he is.

1384
00:53:37,582 --> 00:53:39,183
An absolutely true statement.

1385
00:53:39,217 --> 00:53:41,185
One which you can't
possibly be criticized for.

1386
00:53:41,218 --> 00:53:42,553
- [Tom] Unless you
flub your lines.

1387
00:53:42,587 --> 00:53:43,788
- Even I could fly to Palms,

1388
00:53:43,822 --> 00:53:46,390
and investigate
this ghost story.

1389
00:53:46,423 --> 00:53:48,693
It may hold up the
project a couple of days,

1390
00:53:48,727 --> 00:53:50,995
practically nothing to lose.

1391
00:53:52,763 --> 00:53:54,765
- Well, what do you say?

1392
00:53:54,798 --> 00:53:59,803
- [Joel] It's tempting.
(phone ringing)

1393
00:54:01,005 --> 00:54:02,373
- All right, you win.

1394
00:54:02,407 --> 00:54:03,275
Go on, take off.

1395
00:54:04,775 --> 00:54:06,978
- [Tom] Now we got to
work on those O rings.

1396
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:09,982
- Berringer speaking.

1397
00:54:10,015 --> 00:54:12,817
- [Joel] Yeah, hi, are you
wearing rubber underwear?

1398
00:54:12,850 --> 00:54:14,085
- Yes, Mr. Mydell.

1399
00:54:14,118 --> 00:54:17,455
(plane engines roaring)

1400
00:54:19,857 --> 00:54:20,658
- [Tom] Bye.

1401
00:54:22,560 --> 00:54:24,295
- [Crow] Got to think.

1402
00:54:24,328 --> 00:54:26,063
Must think.

1403
00:54:26,097 --> 00:54:27,865
Must find hand.

1404
00:54:27,898 --> 00:54:30,935
Must comb hair.

1405
00:54:30,968 --> 00:54:34,038
Must get vitales,
rub into scalp.

1406
00:54:34,072 --> 00:54:35,007
Get rid of flaking.

1407
00:54:37,075 --> 00:54:37,842
- Paul?

1408
00:54:37,875 --> 00:54:38,677
- [Tom] Little buddy.

1409
00:54:38,710 --> 00:54:39,543
- Sheriff.

1410
00:54:44,882 --> 00:54:46,483
- You all right?

1411
00:54:46,517 --> 00:54:47,985
- Sure.

1412
00:54:48,018 --> 00:54:48,920
- Where have you been?

1413
00:54:48,953 --> 00:54:50,522
- [Joel] Out by the lagoon.

1414
00:54:50,555 --> 00:54:52,890
- I just took a walk.

1415
00:54:52,923 --> 00:54:54,425
I woke up.

1416
00:54:54,458 --> 00:54:56,393
I guess this thing bothered me
a little more than I thought.

1417
00:54:56,427 --> 00:54:59,063
I just took a walk.

1418
00:54:59,097 --> 00:55:00,332
- But you're filthy dirty.

1419
00:55:00,365 --> 00:55:01,498
- [Tom] Yeah, and
you're stocky, tubby.

1420
00:55:01,532 --> 00:55:02,433
- And you're all sweaty.

1421
00:55:02,467 --> 00:55:04,102
You must've walked awfully hard.

1422
00:55:05,503 --> 00:55:07,272
Now look, Paul, I'm here
because an ambulance driver

1423
00:55:07,305 --> 00:55:08,740
called me.

1424
00:55:08,773 --> 00:55:10,608
He told me a strange
kind of a story.

1425
00:55:10,642 --> 00:55:11,910
Have you got
anything to tell me?

1426
00:55:11,943 --> 00:55:13,512
- [Crow] Just a strange
kind of a story.

1427
00:55:13,545 --> 00:55:14,378
- No sir.

1428
00:55:15,780 --> 00:55:16,948
- All right, I'll tell
you what I want you to do.

1429
00:55:16,982 --> 00:55:18,215
I want you to stick
close to house.

1430
00:55:18,248 --> 00:55:19,717
Now, I wouldn't go out
at all if I were you.

1431
00:55:19,750 --> 00:55:20,652
I don't care what you do,

1432
00:55:20,685 --> 00:55:21,720
but stick close to house.

1433
00:55:21,753 --> 00:55:22,953
Do you understand?

1434
00:55:22,987 --> 00:55:24,188
- Oh, why don't you
go ahead and arrest me

1435
00:55:24,222 --> 00:55:24,855
and get it over with.
- There's no charge

1436
00:55:24,888 --> 00:55:25,457
against you boy.

1437
00:55:25,490 --> 00:55:26,692
Not yet.

1438
00:55:26,725 --> 00:55:27,825
- [Tom] I'm not
a boy, I'm a man.

1439
00:55:29,027 --> 00:55:30,828
- Now I suggest you go
up and get some sleep.

1440
00:55:30,862 --> 00:55:32,630
You've had a hard night.

1441
00:55:32,663 --> 00:55:33,465
- [Crow] Hard day's night.

1442
00:55:33,498 --> 00:55:34,632
- Yes sir.

1443
00:55:38,002 --> 00:55:41,538
(Joel, Tom, Crow groaning)

1444
00:55:41,572 --> 00:55:43,207
- [Tom] Hi, welcome
to your room.

1445
00:55:45,577 --> 00:55:47,412
- [Joel] He's a
rebel without a hand.

1446
00:55:59,090 --> 00:56:01,658
(car starting)

1447
00:56:06,765 --> 00:56:07,998
- [Tom] Dear Kitty,

1448
00:56:08,032 --> 00:56:09,800
by the time you hear
this, I'll be dead.

1449
00:56:10,902 --> 00:56:12,570
- [Joel] Good
morning, Mr. Phelps.

1450
00:56:12,603 --> 00:56:15,773
- I'm telling this,
really, for you.

1451
00:56:15,807 --> 00:56:17,642
(dramatic music)

1452
00:56:17,675 --> 00:56:19,877
Mr. Farnstrom and Marta.

1453
00:56:22,780 --> 00:56:25,683
- [Crow] I would now like
to sing, Cathy's Clown.

1454
00:56:25,717 --> 00:56:27,952
- I know you will both realize

1455
00:56:27,985 --> 00:56:29,087
that it is the truth.

1456
00:56:30,255 --> 00:56:32,290
- [Tom] I have touched
the unclean thing.

1457
00:56:37,895 --> 00:56:39,397
- You were right, Professor.

1458
00:56:39,430 --> 00:56:41,132
- [Joel] Dogs can fly.

1459
00:56:41,165 --> 00:56:42,967
- [Crow] Cold fusion
is impossible.

1460
00:56:43,902 --> 00:56:45,203
- Keep things to myself.

1461
00:56:47,805 --> 00:56:50,642
I tried to be a lone wolf.

1462
00:56:50,675 --> 00:56:52,077
- [Tom] Now I'm a dead duck.

1463
00:56:53,478 --> 00:56:56,613
- I guess I wanted all
the credit for myself.

1464
00:56:58,115 --> 00:56:59,650
Marta and I were swimming.

1465
00:57:02,753 --> 00:57:06,858
And up on the sand we found
something quite interesting.

1466
00:57:08,560 --> 00:57:09,560
It was Lock's.

1467
00:57:11,162 --> 00:57:12,263
Just an arm.

1468
00:57:13,598 --> 00:57:17,002
Had a strange covering.

1469
00:57:19,537 --> 00:57:24,542
I guessed right away it
must've been (mumbling).

1470
00:57:26,410 --> 00:57:27,245
- [Tom] Tripping.

1471
00:57:30,282 --> 00:57:34,318
- [Joel] Remind me not to
drop acid after school.

1472
00:57:34,352 --> 00:57:37,122
(dramatic music)

1473
00:57:39,057 --> 00:57:42,660
- [Tom] He's doing his
Pete Townsend impression.

1474
00:57:42,693 --> 00:57:43,528
There it is.

1475
00:57:44,395 --> 00:57:47,298
People try to put us down.

1476
00:57:47,332 --> 00:57:49,100
- [Joel] It's a close
and play phonograph.

1477
00:57:49,133 --> 00:57:50,235
- [Crow] Drop it.

1478
00:57:50,268 --> 00:57:51,135
- [Tom] Stomps.

1479
00:57:52,170 --> 00:57:54,438
Close it.
Shatters.

1480
00:57:54,472 --> 00:57:56,173
Step on it.
Smashes.

1481
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:03,682
- [Crow] Hey, it's
Timothy Hutton.

1482
00:58:03,715 --> 00:58:06,550
- [Joel] No, it's more
like Adam Ant to me.

1483
00:58:06,583 --> 00:58:07,418
- [Crow] Maybe.

1484
00:58:09,353 --> 00:58:12,457
- [Tom] It's from a place
called unchartered desert isle.

1485
00:58:16,627 --> 00:58:18,262
- [Crow] Dear Skipper,
why haven't you sent help?

1486
00:58:18,295 --> 00:58:20,063
Signed, The Castaways.

1487
00:58:20,097 --> 00:58:21,465
- When'd this
thing arrive, Earl?

1488
00:58:21,498 --> 00:58:22,867
- [Earl] Why, what is it?

1489
00:58:22,900 --> 00:58:24,635
- It's from Washington.

1490
00:58:24,668 --> 00:58:26,470
We asked for a confirmation
on those fingerprints

1491
00:58:26,503 --> 00:58:28,105
at the Hotchkiss house.

1492
00:58:28,138 --> 00:58:30,442
Well, they said they belong
to a guy I never heard of,

1493
00:58:30,475 --> 00:58:31,275
Lockhart.

1494
00:58:31,308 --> 00:58:32,110
- Who?

1495
00:58:32,143 --> 00:58:33,545
- Melvin Lockhart.

1496
00:58:33,578 --> 00:58:34,545
Do you know a Melvin Lockhart?

1497
00:58:34,578 --> 00:58:35,813
- [Joel] June's boy?

1498
00:58:35,847 --> 00:58:36,613
- Anybody in this
town by that name?

1499
00:58:36,647 --> 00:58:37,515
- Never heard of one.

1500
00:58:37,548 --> 00:58:38,448
- [Joel] June's boy?

1501
00:58:38,482 --> 00:58:39,517
- That isn't the half of it.

1502
00:58:39,550 --> 00:58:41,185
They're moving in.

1503
00:58:41,218 --> 00:58:42,353
- Who's moving in?

1504
00:58:42,387 --> 00:58:43,822
- Washington.
- [Joel] June's boy.

1505
00:58:43,855 --> 00:58:45,423
George?
Grover?

1506
00:58:45,457 --> 00:58:46,823
- Great Caesar's ghost.

1507
00:58:46,857 --> 00:58:48,325
- [Crow] Julius?
Augustus?

1508
00:58:48,358 --> 00:58:50,728
- Can you tell me why they
want in on a case like this?

1509
00:58:50,762 --> 00:58:52,163
- [Joel] Curiosity?
Money?

1510
00:58:52,197 --> 00:58:54,232
- A widow lady gets
herself killed.

1511
00:58:54,265 --> 00:58:56,968
A prime suspect, the
only possible suspect,

1512
00:58:57,002 --> 00:58:58,903
I've got right here in my hands.

1513
00:58:58,937 --> 00:59:00,203
(Joel, Tom, and Crow moaning)

1514
00:59:00,238 --> 00:59:03,040
I'm gonna make an arrest
within a couple hours.

1515
00:59:03,073 --> 00:59:03,975
- The kid?

1516
00:59:04,008 --> 00:59:05,208
- Most certainly the kid.

1517
00:59:05,243 --> 00:59:07,078
You know the way
he's been acting.

1518
00:59:07,112 --> 00:59:08,545
- [Crow] Like us, bad.

1519
00:59:08,580 --> 00:59:09,813
- He's the only one who
could possibly have done it.

1520
00:59:09,847 --> 00:59:11,082
Now they come up with
a man named Lockhart.

1521
00:59:11,115 --> 00:59:12,017
- [Tom] June's boy.

1522
00:59:12,050 --> 00:59:13,350
- And they're taking over.

1523
00:59:14,485 --> 00:59:15,853
Earl, I'll tell you
what's happened.

1524
00:59:15,887 --> 00:59:17,422
They've got so many
fingerprints in Washington,

1525
00:59:17,455 --> 00:59:18,923
they've got 'em all mixed up.

1526
00:59:20,792 --> 00:59:22,593
Well, what's the use.

1527
00:59:22,627 --> 00:59:24,028
We can't buck 'em, not yet.

1528
00:59:24,062 --> 00:59:26,397
(Joel, Tom, and Crow grunting,
coughing, and whistling)

1529
00:59:26,430 --> 00:59:28,700
They also want to have us
put a guard on the house.

1530
00:59:28,733 --> 00:59:30,735
Nobody gets in.

1531
00:59:30,768 --> 00:59:32,437
- Want me to go over
and sit on the place.

1532
00:59:32,470 --> 00:59:33,370
- Yeah, you better.

1533
00:59:33,403 --> 00:59:34,905
- [Tom] Better bring a cushion.

1534
00:59:34,938 --> 00:59:37,142
- While you're there, keep
an eye on young Lawrence.

1535
00:59:37,175 --> 00:59:38,810
I suppose I should've picked
him up long before this,

1536
00:59:38,843 --> 00:59:40,312
but I just didn't want
him to have a record

1537
00:59:40,345 --> 00:59:41,245
until I was 100% sure.

1538
00:59:41,278 --> 00:59:43,080
- [Joel] He's such a sweet kid.

1539
00:59:43,113 --> 00:59:44,748
- And then after
those Washington boys

1540
00:59:44,782 --> 00:59:45,950
get through fooling around,

1541
00:59:45,983 --> 00:59:47,085
I'm afraid he's
still gonna be it,

1542
00:59:47,118 --> 00:59:48,620
and I'd bet a hundred
dollars on it.

1543
00:59:48,653 --> 00:59:50,053
- Okay, I'll get
right over there.

1544
00:59:50,087 --> 00:59:50,922
- All right.

1545
00:59:56,060 --> 00:59:57,395
- [Crow] Damn MaryAnn.

1546
00:59:57,428 --> 00:59:59,997
(upbeat music)

1547
01:00:06,470 --> 01:00:07,738
- [Tom] Clearasil.

1548
01:00:07,772 --> 01:00:09,040
Clearasil.

1549
01:00:10,007 --> 01:00:11,442
Oxy, Oxy.

1550
01:00:14,212 --> 01:00:15,347
- Yes, what can I do for you?

1551
01:00:15,380 --> 01:00:16,847
- Afternoon, Sheriff,
I'm Steve Curan,

1552
01:00:16,880 --> 01:00:18,783
this is Doc Weitzberg, and
we're from Washington, D.C.

1553
01:00:18,817 --> 01:00:20,017
- [Joel] We talk real fast.

1554
01:00:20,050 --> 01:00:21,885
- Well, you government
boys really work fast.

1555
01:00:21,918 --> 01:00:23,020
You got here in a hurry.

1556
01:00:23,053 --> 01:00:24,188
Let me get you some coffee.

1557
01:00:24,222 --> 01:00:25,122
- Well, thank you, no.
- No thank you.

1558
01:00:26,357 --> 01:00:28,927
- Say tell me, who's
this man Lockhart?

1559
01:00:28,960 --> 01:00:29,860
- Wait a minute, let's
not get started off

1560
01:00:29,893 --> 01:00:31,495
on the wrong foot here, Sheriff.

1561
01:00:31,528 --> 01:00:33,865
- Yeah, I know, you ask
the questions, I don't.

1562
01:00:35,333 --> 01:00:37,335
Well you boys are from
the bureau, aren't you?

1563
01:00:37,368 --> 01:00:38,568
- Well that's just it, Sheriff,

1564
01:00:38,602 --> 01:00:40,805
we're from Space Operations.

1565
01:00:40,838 --> 01:00:43,273
- Space Operations?

1566
01:00:43,307 --> 01:00:44,675
Oh now, wait a minute boys.

1567
01:00:44,708 --> 01:00:48,512
This is Palms, California,
population 2306.

1568
01:00:48,545 --> 01:00:49,813
- [Joel] We don't
have space here.

1569
01:00:49,847 --> 01:00:51,115
- We know all about
that, Sheriff.

1570
01:00:51,148 --> 01:00:52,950
We just want to ask you
a few questions is all.

1571
01:00:52,983 --> 01:00:55,318
- About this very man, Lockhart.

1572
01:00:55,353 --> 01:00:56,853
Has he been seen?

1573
01:00:56,887 --> 01:00:58,522
- Well, I don't know who you
boys are, or why you're here.

1574
01:00:58,555 --> 01:01:00,392
As far as I'm concerned,
you've got no authority at all

1575
01:01:00,425 --> 01:01:01,592
to ask me questions.

1576
01:01:01,625 --> 01:01:03,027
- Now wait a minute,
we didn't come out here

1577
01:01:03,060 --> 01:01:04,562
to get pushed around.
- Now, Steve, just a minute.

1578
01:01:04,595 --> 01:01:05,530
Sheriff, as a matter of fact,
all we want is your permission

1579
01:01:05,563 --> 01:01:07,498
to go through the house.

1580
01:01:07,532 --> 01:01:08,933
- Nobody goes through the house.

1581
01:01:08,967 --> 01:01:11,302
Not til the boys from
the Bureau get here.

1582
01:01:11,302 --> 01:01:14,105
And I've got an armed guard
that says nobody does.

1583
01:01:14,138 --> 01:01:16,207
- [Joel] Yowser.

1584
01:01:16,240 --> 01:01:18,342
- [Tom] Oh yeah, well
we know Buzz Aldrin.

1585
01:01:19,943 --> 01:01:20,812
- [Crow] Fatty.

1586
01:01:25,517 --> 01:01:28,152
(Crow grunting)

1587
01:01:29,420 --> 01:01:31,222
Oh, that's not so bad.

1588
01:01:31,255 --> 01:01:32,623
I don't really have acne anyway.

1589
01:01:32,657 --> 01:01:33,623
I can go to that dance.

1590
01:01:33,657 --> 01:01:36,327
It's not a pimple, it's my nose.

1591
01:01:36,360 --> 01:01:37,528
What was I thinking of?

1592
01:01:39,630 --> 01:01:41,032
- [Joel] Hubcap, duck.

1593
01:01:44,868 --> 01:01:46,570
- Oh, we just talked
to the sheriff.

1594
01:01:46,637 --> 01:01:47,872
- Yeah, he called me.

1595
01:01:47,938 --> 01:01:49,340
- He didn't change
his mind, did he?

1596
01:01:49,373 --> 01:01:50,942
- Nope.

1597
01:01:50,942 --> 01:01:53,010
- Well, do you mind if we
just look around outside?

1598
01:01:54,478 --> 01:01:56,247
- Nothing to see,
but help yourself.

1599
01:01:58,950 --> 01:02:00,383
- Do you have any theories?

1600
01:02:00,418 --> 01:02:02,087
- Yeah, somebody killed her.

1601
01:02:02,120 --> 01:02:03,353
That's my theory.

1602
01:02:03,387 --> 01:02:05,288
- [Tom] Norman, get away
from the window, Norman.

1603
01:02:06,790 --> 01:02:09,293
- Well, Steve, I guess we
might as well be on our way.

1604
01:02:09,327 --> 01:02:10,462
- Doc, you notice,

1605
01:02:10,495 --> 01:02:12,062
they don't build houses
like this anymore.

1606
01:02:13,597 --> 01:02:14,865
- [Crow] Kick Sheriff.

1607
01:02:16,067 --> 01:02:17,268
Kick him.

1608
01:02:17,302 --> 01:02:19,637
- Oh no, they sure
don't, do they.

1609
01:02:21,105 --> 01:02:22,140
- [Tom] No they don't make
houses like this anymore.

1610
01:02:22,173 --> 01:02:25,208
(Tom grunting)

1611
01:02:25,242 --> 01:02:26,843
- Well, we'll see you around.

1612
01:02:26,877 --> 01:02:27,878
- See ya later.
- Yeah.

1613
01:02:27,912 --> 01:02:28,780
- [Tom] Good bye.

1614
01:02:32,750 --> 01:02:35,318
(car starting)

1615
01:02:37,922 --> 01:02:39,023
- [Joel] Your muffler.

1616
01:02:49,433 --> 01:02:51,802
- [Tom] Rapunzel, Rapunzel,
let down your hand.

1617
01:02:53,303 --> 01:02:55,740
- [Crow] Yeah, I think they're
gonna do a scene from Cyrano.

1618
01:03:02,880 --> 01:03:04,482
- Whatever we gotta get done,
we better get done fast.

1619
01:03:04,515 --> 01:03:06,750
That cold fish could be
around here any second.

1620
01:03:06,783 --> 01:03:09,287
- [Crow] Hey, I think somebody
just called me a cold fish.

1621
01:03:09,320 --> 01:03:10,688
Why?

1622
01:03:10,722 --> 01:03:11,955
- Did you distinguish whether
it was a man or woman?

1623
01:03:11,988 --> 01:03:12,923
- It was a pretty
quick glance, Doc.

1624
01:03:12,957 --> 01:03:14,325
I'm not sure.

1625
01:03:14,358 --> 01:03:15,192
- Look Steve.

1626
01:03:16,460 --> 01:03:17,595
- [Tom] Incoming, duck.

1627
01:03:22,333 --> 01:03:25,435
- [Joel] Now the
gates over here.

1628
01:03:25,468 --> 01:03:27,272
Hey, how'd you get over there?

1629
01:03:28,338 --> 01:03:30,307
Geez, ow, down there.

1630
01:03:30,340 --> 01:03:31,642
Could you get that for me?

1631
01:03:34,912 --> 01:03:35,747
- It's a note.

1632
01:03:37,047 --> 01:03:39,017
Come back tonight, after dark.

1633
01:03:39,050 --> 01:03:40,785
- Back door'll be unlocked.

1634
01:03:40,818 --> 01:03:42,753
I have something
important to tell you.

1635
01:03:43,620 --> 01:03:45,055
- [Tom] Wear something sheer.

1636
01:03:46,123 --> 01:03:47,292
- No signature.

1637
01:03:47,325 --> 01:03:48,192
- Well, I didn't
have a date tonight.

1638
01:03:48,225 --> 01:03:49,060
Did you?

1639
01:03:50,493 --> 01:03:51,995
- Well, it looks like we
both have now, doesn't it?

1640
01:03:53,130 --> 01:03:54,298
- [Crow] Let's see,
something sheer.

1641
01:03:54,332 --> 01:03:55,667
How about that pink
chiffon number.

1642
01:03:55,700 --> 01:03:57,735
Oh no wait, you're using that.

1643
01:03:57,768 --> 01:03:59,470
What about that taffeta,
that tea length,

1644
01:03:59,503 --> 01:04:01,738
you know, the one I wore
to the IGY convention.

1645
01:04:01,772 --> 01:04:03,908
You know, the night I went
home with Eureka Garren.

1646
01:04:03,942 --> 01:04:05,342
You remember?

1647
01:04:05,375 --> 01:04:07,945
(car starting)

1648
01:04:12,717 --> 01:04:14,218
- Hey.

1649
01:04:14,252 --> 01:04:16,520
- [Joel] Hey, wait a minute,
stop or I'll say hey again.

1650
01:04:19,057 --> 01:04:19,890
Ouch.

1651
01:04:28,132 --> 01:04:29,500
- Paul?

1652
01:04:29,533 --> 01:04:30,968
- [Tom] How'd she get in?

1653
01:04:31,002 --> 01:04:33,603
- [Crow] She found the hole
in the security curtain.

1654
01:04:40,477 --> 01:04:41,312
- [Tom] Paul?

1655
01:04:45,583 --> 01:04:46,383
- Paul.

1656
01:04:49,253 --> 01:04:51,455
- What are you doing here?

1657
01:04:51,488 --> 01:04:52,390
Get out.

1658
01:04:52,423 --> 01:04:53,690
- Paul, are you all right?

1659
01:04:55,125 --> 01:04:57,862
I read in the paper about
poor Mrs. Hotchkiss.

1660
01:04:57,895 --> 01:04:58,930
It's such a terrible thing.

1661
01:04:58,963 --> 01:05:00,765
- Never mind.

1662
01:05:00,798 --> 01:05:01,632
Get out of here.

1663
01:05:02,567 --> 01:05:05,302
- Paul, that thing on the beach.

1664
01:05:06,470 --> 01:05:08,305
I just can't get
it out of my mind.

1665
01:05:08,338 --> 01:05:09,740
- [Tom] Well I can't
get it out of my house.

1666
01:05:09,773 --> 01:05:10,608
- No.

1667
01:05:11,842 --> 01:05:13,810
Get out.

1668
01:05:13,843 --> 01:05:14,845
- Why are you staying here?

1669
01:05:14,878 --> 01:05:15,780
It's no good.

1670
01:05:15,813 --> 01:05:17,915
You can come to our house.

1671
01:05:17,948 --> 01:05:19,617
- Marta, please.

1672
01:05:21,818 --> 01:05:23,053
- Marta, what are
you doing here?

1673
01:05:23,087 --> 01:05:24,855
- Harrison, get her
out of this house!

1674
01:05:24,888 --> 01:05:26,057
Just get her out!

1675
01:05:26,090 --> 01:05:28,525
- You just get
back to your room.

1676
01:05:28,558 --> 01:05:29,560
- [Tom] And no playing
with your hand,

1677
01:05:29,593 --> 01:05:30,560
and no supper.

1678
01:05:30,593 --> 01:05:31,962
- Wait a minute.

1679
01:05:31,995 --> 01:05:33,063
I've a right to see Paul.

1680
01:05:33,097 --> 01:05:34,865
I'm a friend of his.

1681
01:05:35,833 --> 01:05:37,802
- Get her out of this house.

1682
01:05:37,835 --> 01:05:39,837
- [Crow] I think he wants
her out of the house.

1683
01:05:39,870 --> 01:05:41,438
- Come on, Marta, out, come on.

1684
01:05:43,773 --> 01:05:44,675
- [Tom] Bye Marta.

1685
01:05:45,910 --> 01:05:48,345
- And don't let her come back!

1686
01:05:48,378 --> 01:05:50,313
- [Joel] Think he's been
watching James Dean movies,

1687
01:05:50,347 --> 01:05:51,182
you guys?

1688
01:05:52,248 --> 01:05:53,017
What is that?

1689
01:05:53,050 --> 01:05:54,385
An ice cream place?

1690
01:05:54,418 --> 01:05:55,853
- [Tom] Coffee house, I think.

1691
01:05:55,887 --> 01:05:56,687
- [Crow] Bagel shop.

1692
01:05:56,720 --> 01:05:57,622
- [Joel] Pizza place.

1693
01:05:57,655 --> 01:05:59,123
- [Tom] Oh, no, it's a mortuary.

1694
01:05:59,157 --> 01:06:00,290
Silly us.

1695
01:06:00,323 --> 01:06:01,658
- [Crow] Why didn't they say?

1696
01:06:01,692 --> 01:06:02,860
- [Joel] Is that a peace sign?

1697
01:06:02,893 --> 01:06:04,395
- [Tom] Nope, I hope
this movie's over soon.

1698
01:06:04,428 --> 01:06:06,597
- I didn't know she had any
relatives back east at all.

1699
01:06:06,630 --> 01:06:07,898
I know she used to
speak of an uncle,

1700
01:06:07,932 --> 01:06:09,100
but he was in England
or some place.

1701
01:06:09,133 --> 01:06:11,068
Where'd you say you
were from again?

1702
01:06:11,102 --> 01:06:12,870
- Pennsylvania.
- New York.

1703
01:06:12,903 --> 01:06:13,803
- Oh, he's from New York.

1704
01:06:13,837 --> 01:06:15,172
- And he's from Pennsylvania.

1705
01:06:15,205 --> 01:06:17,207
We heard about the
tragedy, and flew out here.

1706
01:06:17,240 --> 01:06:18,175
- I see.

1707
01:06:18,208 --> 01:06:20,010
Well, here she is.

1708
01:06:20,043 --> 01:06:21,578
You can take a look if you like.

1709
01:06:21,612 --> 01:06:23,080
Really looks very well.

1710
01:06:23,113 --> 01:06:26,283
Of course, we got the body
quite soon after the crime.

1711
01:06:26,317 --> 01:06:28,852
And there weren't any marks,
except the ones on her throat.

1712
01:06:28,885 --> 01:06:30,087
- [Tom] And the ones we added.

1713
01:06:30,120 --> 01:06:31,755
- You know, sometimes
in cases like this.

1714
01:06:31,788 --> 01:06:33,357
- [Crow] We put
turtlenecks on 'em.

1715
01:06:33,390 --> 01:06:35,827
- Looks very well, I think.

1716
01:06:40,563 --> 01:06:42,433
- Yes, Steve, yes.

1717
01:06:45,737 --> 01:06:46,537
- What's he doing?

1718
01:06:46,570 --> 01:06:47,472
What is that thing?

1719
01:06:47,505 --> 01:06:48,405
- [Joel] It's a dust buster.

1720
01:06:48,438 --> 01:06:50,007
- Wards off evil spirits.

1721
01:06:50,040 --> 01:06:51,342
- Oh, I see.

1722
01:06:51,375 --> 01:06:52,910
- There's absolutely no doubt.

1723
01:06:52,943 --> 01:06:55,612
Thanks very much,
we're satisfied.

1724
01:06:55,645 --> 01:06:58,548
- And you're absolutely
right, she looks very well.

1725
01:06:58,582 --> 01:07:01,152
- [Crow] Which is more than
I can say for you, baldy.

1726
01:07:04,522 --> 01:07:05,488
- [Tom] Hey, come back.

1727
01:07:05,522 --> 01:07:06,957
Is that really a dust buster?

1728
01:07:07,958 --> 01:07:10,693
(dramatic music)

1729
01:07:14,932 --> 01:07:16,333
- Hello?

1730
01:07:16,367 --> 01:07:17,200
- [Tom] Hi.

1731
01:07:18,835 --> 01:07:19,903
- Hello.

1732
01:07:19,937 --> 01:07:20,738
- [Tom] Hi.

1733
01:07:20,772 --> 01:07:21,938
- Anybody here?

1734
01:07:21,972 --> 01:07:22,873
- [Joel] Yeah,
us, we're in here.

1735
01:07:22,907 --> 01:07:24,508
- It looks like we're alone.

1736
01:07:24,542 --> 01:07:25,575
- [Crow] At last.

1737
01:07:26,743 --> 01:07:28,278
- I wonder where our
little friend is.

1738
01:07:28,312 --> 01:07:29,213
- [Joel] He's right here.

1739
01:07:29,247 --> 01:07:30,147
- If he was watching, all right,

1740
01:07:30,180 --> 01:07:31,348
he'll be down any minute.

1741
01:07:31,382 --> 01:07:33,683
- [Tom] I'll be
your little friend.

1742
01:07:33,717 --> 01:07:35,718
Oh, it's the crawling bladder.

1743
01:07:35,753 --> 01:07:37,522
- If we could only
turn on a light.

1744
01:07:37,555 --> 01:07:39,357
- [Joel] These guys are
the greatest comedy team

1745
01:07:39,390 --> 01:07:41,192
since Willie Aames
and Scott Baio.

1746
01:07:41,225 --> 01:07:44,695
- We gotta keep this flashlight
out of the windowsill.

1747
01:07:44,728 --> 01:07:45,528
Steve.

1748
01:07:45,562 --> 01:07:46,363
- What?

1749
01:07:46,397 --> 01:07:47,197
- Give me the light.

1750
01:07:47,230 --> 01:07:48,098
Put it on the gauge.

1751
01:07:49,833 --> 01:07:51,102
- What do you see?

1752
01:07:51,135 --> 01:07:52,503
- Well there's an indication.

1753
01:07:52,537 --> 01:07:54,772
It's faint, but it's definite.

1754
01:07:54,805 --> 01:07:57,273
- [Joel] This corn
relish is radio active.

1755
01:07:57,307 --> 01:07:59,243
- Steve, keep it over here.

1756
01:08:05,415 --> 01:08:06,583
- What's it reading?

1757
01:08:07,685 --> 01:08:09,287
- This is very strange.

1758
01:08:09,320 --> 01:08:10,753
- What's that?

1759
01:08:10,788 --> 01:08:11,888
- Well there's a very
strong reading right here,

1760
01:08:11,922 --> 01:08:13,590
on top of the shelf.

1761
01:08:13,623 --> 01:08:16,193
Nothing above or below.

1762
01:08:16,227 --> 01:08:18,262
- That's ridiculous, Doc.

1763
01:08:18,295 --> 01:08:19,397
No man could be
up on that shelf.

1764
01:08:19,430 --> 01:08:21,632
It would never hold him.

1765
01:08:21,665 --> 01:08:23,967
- [Tom] Just part
of a man, maybe.

1766
01:08:24,000 --> 01:08:25,268
Just a hunch.

1767
01:08:25,302 --> 01:08:27,237
- Lockhart's
fingerprints, remember.

1768
01:08:28,372 --> 01:08:29,840
- Well perhaps we
could trace the trail

1769
